/* GENERATED by tools/import_book.js from the finished manuscript of Book 4. Story text is the author's, verbatim; do not edit here. */
(function(){ G.HFQ.addSeriesText("htfa-4",{"chapters":[{"t":"Prologue — The Road That Disappeared","blocks":[{"p":"The first time I got lost that summer, I was standing in my own kitchen."},{"p":"I had a map spread across the table, a pencil behind my ear, and a bowl of cereal going soft beside my elbow. I knew exactly where I wanted to go. That was the problem. I had drawn a straight line there without thinking about what lay between."},{"p":"Dad came in carrying a basket of laundry and stopped behind my chair."},{"p":"“Planning an escape?” he asked."},{"p":"“Dayton, Ohio.”"},{"p":"“That’s a long escape.”"},{"p":"“It’s a family trip. You approved it.”"},{"p":"“I approved visiting your mother’s cousin and the aviation museum. I don’t remember approving you as navigator.”"},{"p":"He leaned over the map. My line crossed the mountains in West Virginia, then continued northwest as if mountains were no different from the blue squares on graph paper."},{"p":"“What’s that?” he asked, pointing to a dark patch."},{"p":"“A mountain.”"},{"p":"“And that?”"},{"p":"“Another mountain.”"},{"p":"“Interesting. You seem to have discovered several.”"},{"p":"“Airplanes fly over mountains, Dad.”"},{"p":"“Cars don’t. Not usually.”"},{"p":"I turned the map around. He was right. I’d been so interested in the direct route an airplane might take that I’d forgotten we were driving. Our car needed actual roads. They bent around ridges, threaded through valleys, and sometimes doubled back just to annoy anyone holding a map."},{"p":"Dad put down the laundry basket and pulled out a chair."},{"p":"“Want to show me the route you meant to make?”"},{"p":"I opened my mouth to defend the one I’d already drawn. Then I remembered how that usually went."},{"p":"“I might need another pencil,” I said."},{"p":"He pushed mine back toward me. “Try an eraser first.”"},{"p":"Outside, a delivery truck turned onto our street and vanished behind the neighbor’s trees. For a few seconds I could hear it without seeing it."},{"p":"That bothered me more than it should have."},{"p":"A year ago, I would have thought the point of a map was getting from one place to another. Now I could see a hundred ways to go wrong in between. A straight line made them all look easy."},{"p":"I took the eraser off my pencil and rubbed away the straight line."},{"p":"It left a gray streak behind."},{"p":"Dad watched me work. “You know,” he said, “there’s nothing wrong with a shortcut.”"},{"p":"“Until it takes you somewhere you can’t go.”"},{"p":"“I was going to say until your mother finds out you skipped the lunch stop she planned. But yours works too.”"},{"p":"Mom had circled that lunch stop in red. Twice."},{"p":"I drew the new route around it."}]},{"t":"Chapter One — The Fold in the Chart","blocks":[{"p":"The chart was bigger than my desk and determined to stay that way."},{"p":"I opened one corner. Another corner folded itself shut. I held down the middle with my notebook, and the right side slid off the table onto Beckett’s shoe."},{"p":"“Your map’s attacking me,” he said."},{"p":"“It’s a chart.”"},{"p":"“It’s a chart attacking me.”"},{"p":"Rafe reached across and flattened the paper with both hands. “I can see why pilots use tablets.”"},{"p":"“Pilots also learn to read charts,” Imani said. She put her water bottle on the corner nearest her. “And your tablet can run out of battery.”"},{"p":"“My phone’s at eighty-six percent.”"},{"p":"“Your phone isn’t a flight plan.”"},{"p":"“Not with that attitude.”"},{"p":"We were back in Mara Ellery’s classroom at Hampton Roads Executive Airport. The air conditioner made a faint rattling noise, and somebody in the hangar had been running a vacuum for ten minutes without seeming to move it anywhere. On the whiteboard Mara had written three words:"},{"p":"**WHERE ARE YOU?**"},{"p":"Underneath she’d written three more:"},{"p":"**HOW DO YOU KNOW?**"},{"p":"I thought the second question was unnecessary. I’d spent two summers learning airports, winds, weather reports, and enough instrument names to make dinner conversations uncomfortable. If somebody asked where I was, I could point to a chart."},{"p":"Mara walked behind our table and looked at the mess of paper, pencils, and Rafe’s half-eaten granola bar."},{"p":"“Before we begin,” she said, “how many of you have used a map app to get somewhere?”"},{"p":"All four hands went up."},{"p":"“How many have missed a turn even with the directions talking to you?”"},{"p":"Rafe raised both hands. Beckett raised one finger. Imani glanced at me."},{"p":"“I took a wrong exit once,” I admitted."},{"p":"“Once,” Beckett repeated."},{"p":"“That you know about.”"},{"p":"Mara smiled and drew a little triangle on the board. “Navigation starts with knowing your position. The airplane doesn’t care where you intended to be. It is wherever it actually is.”"},{"p":"She handed out a fictional practice route. We weren’t filing a flight plan or flying that morning. We were learning to read a sectional chart and to ask the kinds of questions that made a route more than a pencil mark."},{"p":"“Find Hampton Roads Executive,” she said. “Then identify two landmarks that could help you recognize the area from above.”"},{"p":"I found KPVG quickly. Then I noticed how crowded the chart was. Thin lines, thick lines, blue numbers, magenta numbers, circles, boxes, and symbols I hadn’t seen since last year’s lessons. Some marks were about airports. Others described airspace, obstacles, or information that could matter even if you never intended to land nearby."},{"p":"“There’s a river,” I said."},{"p":"“There are several,” Imani replied."},{"p":"“A very helpful river.”"},{"p":"“What’s distinctive about it?”"},{"p":"I looked closer. The waterway bent around a patch of land before continuing toward a larger stretch of water. I pointed to the bend."},{"p":"“That shape. And the highway crossing farther west.”"},{"p":"Mara nodded. “Better. If you say ‘the river,’ you might recognize the wrong river. A checkpoint should be distinctive enough to identify, not just easy to see.”"},{"p":"Beckett drew a tiny car on the highway. He added smoke coming out of it."},{"p":"“Why’s the car on fire?” I asked."},{"p":"“It’s not on fire. It’s moving fast.”"},{"p":"“Then why is the smoke coming from the roof?”"},{"p":"“Aerodynamics.”"},{"p":"Imani reached over and drew a little tow truck behind it. Beckett stared at her for a second, then laughed."},{"p":"Mara gave us a moment before continuing. “A sectional chart gives you information. It doesn’t tell you what you forgot to check. What else would you check before a real flight?”"},{"p":"“Weather,” Imani said."},{"p":"“Fuel,” Beckett added."},{"p":"“Airspace,” Rafe said. “And whether the airport is open.”"},{"p":"“Current notices and chart information,” Mara said. “Aircraft performance, terrain, obstacles, suitable alternatives. You don’t need to memorize the whole country today. You do need to learn not to ignore the things you haven’t looked at.”"},{"p":"I studied the route again. There was a cluster of obstacle symbols near one proposed checkpoint. I had been looking for roads and rivers so hard that I’d barely noticed them."},{"p":"“If we’re following a road,” I asked, “how do we know we haven’t drifted toward a different one?”"},{"p":"“Good question,” Mara said. “How would you figure it out?”"},{"p":"Rafe leaned back. “Ask the road to show identification.”"},{"p":"“The road would fail,” Beckett said. “No wallet.”"},{"p":"“Seriously,” Mara said, though she was smiling."},{"p":"I traced the route with my pencil. “Check more than one thing. The road and a river crossing, maybe. Or the shape of a town.”"},{"p":"“Exactly. A single landmark can fool you. A combination is more useful. And when what you see doesn’t match what you planned, don’t make the landscape fit your guess. Recheck your position.”"},{"p":"I wrote that down."},{"p":"The classroom door opened. A boy about eleven stepped inside holding a folder against his chest. I recognized him from the open house. His name was Owen, and he’d once asked whether a Cessna could fly upside down. Mara had answered carefully, which was more than I could say for Rafe."},{"p":"“Sorry,” Owen said. “The office said you might have the spare rulers.”"},{"p":"Mara pointed to the cabinet. Owen walked over, then paused at our table."},{"p":"“Why’s your map so wrinkled?”"},{"p":"“Because Lincoln folded it,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“I unfolded it.”"},{"p":"“That’s what you call this?”"},{"p":"Owen picked up one loose corner and looked at the markings. “What’s the big blue number?”"},{"p":"I opened my mouth, then stopped. The chart had several kinds of blue numbers, and I didn’t know which one he meant."},{"p":"“Show me,” I said."},{"p":"He pointed. I checked the legend instead of guessing. We worked it out together while the others returned to their route."},{"p":"Owen grinned when he understood. “So it’s not a secret code.”"},{"p":"“Some of it feels like one,” I said."},{"p":"He took his rulers and left. Beckett watched the door close."},{"p":"“You didn’t make up an answer,” he said."},{"p":"“Thank you for noticing my incredible bravery.”"},{"p":"“I mean it. Old Lincoln would have invented an answer and argued about it for ten minutes.”"},{"p":"“Old Lincoln sounds exhausting.”"},{"p":"“He was,” Imani said."},{"p":"I looked at her. She kept working, but there was a little smile at the corner of her mouth."},{"p":"That was enough for me."},{"p":"By the end of class, our practice route had changed three times. We’d moved one checkpoint, crossed out another, and marked a place where we’d need to pay closer attention. The paper looked worse than when we’d started."},{"p":"Mara studied it. “Tell me why you changed the route.”"},{"p":"I could have said because she told us to. That was the easy answer."},{"p":"“We found things we hadn’t considered,” I said. “And one checkpoint looked clearer on the chart than it probably would from the air.”"},{"p":"“Good. The goal wasn’t to defend your first line.”"},{"p":"Beckett folded his arms. “Could the goal be to learn how to fold the chart?”"},{"p":"“That,” Mara said, “may take the rest of the summer.”"},{"p":"We all tried at once. The paper snapped open again like a sail."},{"p":"Rafe ducked."},{"p":"I thought of Dad at the kitchen table, pointing out the mountains my pencil had flown straight over. It was annoying how often he turned out to be right."},{"p":"I put my hand on the chart before it could hit Beckett in the face."},{"p":"“Maybe,” I said, “we should find the fold lines first.”"}]},{"t":"Chapter Two — A Town with Two Names","blocks":[{"p":"The mistake began with a water tower."},{"p":"I should have known better. Water towers had been getting me into trouble since the foggy morning at the start of last year, when I couldn’t see the one near our neighborhood and had to admit my phone’s sunshine icon wasn’t telling the whole story."},{"p":"This one was different. It was drawn on a practice map Mara had given us, beside a town with a name that sounded familiar. I circled it and wrote CHECKPOINT in capital letters."},{"p":"Imani leaned over my shoulder. “Which town?”"},{"p":"I told her."},{"p":"“There are two with that name on this worksheet.”"},{"p":"“No, there aren’t.”"},{"p":"She slid a finger along the bottom edge of the map. There it was: another town with the same name, farther west beside a different highway."},{"p":"I stared at it."},{"p":"“That’s ridiculous,” I said."},{"p":"“Tell the towns,” she replied."},{"p":"We were working in pairs on a simulated navigation challenge. Each team had a fictional airplane, a departure time, and several checkpoints. Mara had warned us not to use the worksheet for real-world navigation; it was designed to make us think, not to replace current charts or official flight planning."},{"p":"Beckett and Rafe were across the room, arguing about whether a grain elevator looked more like a silo or an enormous soup can."},{"p":"“You were very sure,” Imani said."},{"p":"“I noticed.”"},{"p":"“That’s what worried me.”"},{"p":"She didn’t sound angry. That was worse, somehow. I thought about the weeks when we’d barely spoken after I’d shared something private about her grandmother. We’d worked hard to get past that. I didn’t want another small mistake to become a contest over who was right."},{"p":"“Okay,” I said. “I picked the wrong one.”"},{"p":"She handed me the eraser."},{"p":"“I wasn’t going to make you say it twice.”"},{"p":"We checked the two towns against the other landmarks. One had a road bending north of a lake. The other had a rail line and a river crossing. On the map, their names were identical. Their surroundings weren’t."},{"p":"“What if you didn’t notice until you were flying?” I asked."},{"p":"Imani considered it. “You wouldn’t just keep going because the town’s name matched.”"},{"p":"“Right. But what would you do?”"},{"p":"We called Mara over."},{"p":"She listened without interrupting, then pulled up a chair. “First, you’d maintain safe control of the airplane. Then you’d work to determine where you actually were using appropriate references and navigation resources. If you couldn’t establish your position, you’d ask for help early.”"},{"p":"“Like from air traffic control?” I asked."},{"p":"“When available and appropriate, yes. Pilots can request assistance. The important thing is not to let embarrassment keep you from using it.”"},{"p":"I looked at the water tower again. I could imagine how easy it would be to see something familiar, feel relieved, and stop checking."},{"p":"“We were only wrong on paper,” I said."},{"p":"“That’s a very good place to discover an error,” Mara replied."},{"p":"At lunch, Dad texted a photograph of the route he’d printed for our Ohio trip. Mom had written"},{"p":"REST STOP? across the middle in thick red marker. Underneath, Dad had added ONLY IF THERE ARE BATHROOMS."},{"p":"I showed it to Imani."},{"p":"“Your parents are planning a military operation,” she said."},{"p":"“It’s a nine-hour car ride. We might need one.”"},{"p":"“Are you bringing snacks?”"},{"p":"“Dad’s packing them.”"},{"p":"“Then you’re definitely going to need an alternate.”"},{"p":"I laughed so hard I nearly dropped my sandwich."},{"p":"That afternoon, Mara had us compare our routes. Beckett’s had an excellent series of checkpoints but a scribbled note that read VERIFY THE SOUP CAN. Rafe’s had a carefully calculated heading beside a drawing of a tiny airplane wearing sunglasses. Ours had two erased circles around the towns."},{"p":"“Tell us what went wrong,” Mara said."},{"p":"I stood up with the worksheet. My ears felt hot, but not as hot as they would have a year earlier."},{"p":"“I chose a checkpoint because I recognized the name,” I said. “I didn’t confirm the surrounding landmarks. Imani found another town with the same name. We corrected it before moving on.”"},{"p":"“What would you do differently next time?”"},{"p":"“Check at least two independent clues. And not get attached to the first answer.”"},{"p":"Beckett raised his hand."},{"p":"“Yes?” Mara said."},{"p":"“Can I recommend that the towns change their names?”"},{"p":"“You may write to them.”"},{"p":"“I will. Strongly.”"},{"p":"After class, we walked toward the parking lot together. A small airplane passed overhead, its wings catching the afternoon light. I followed it with my eyes until the hangar roof hid it."},{"p":"“You know what’s weird?” I said."},{"p":"“The way Rafe eats oranges without peeling them?” Beckett asked."},{"p":"“I did that once,” Rafe said."},{"p":"“Once was plenty.”"},{"p":"“No,” I said. “A map can tell you where you want to go, but it can’t promise you’ll recognize it when you get there.”"},{"p":"Imani adjusted the strap of her bag. “That’s why you keep checking.”"},{"p":"“And why you don’t pretend you’re somewhere you’re not,” I said."},{"p":"She glanced at me. “You could put that in your notebook.”"},{"p":"“You think?”"},{"p":"“Sure. Then maybe you’ll remember it next time you circle the wrong town.”"},{"p":"“You really aren’t going to let that go.”"},{"p":"“Not for at least a week.”"},{"p":"We reached the curb. Dad’s car was already there, and he was holding a folded paper map against the steering wheel, trying to read it through his glasses."},{"p":"“Please tell me he’s not driving like that,” Imani said."},{"p":"“He’s parked.”"},{"p":"“Good. Because I was about to request assistance from air traffic control.”"},{"p":"I opened the passenger door. Dad looked up."},{"p":"“How was navigation?”"},{"p":"“I got lost in a town I never visited.”"},{"p":"“Impressive.”"},{"p":"“Then I figured out where I was.”"},{"p":"Dad folded his map. It took him one smooth motion."},{"p":"I stared. “How did you do that?”"},{"p":"“Years of practice,” he said, starting the engine. “Also, this one’s a road map.”"},{"p":"I looked back at my friends. Rafe waved. Beckett made a dramatic airplane gesture with both arms. Imani pointed at her eyes, then at the road, as though reminding me to pay attention."},{"p":"I buckled my seat belt."},{"p":"For once, I did."}]},{"t":"Chapter Three — The Checkpoint That Moved","blocks":[{"p":"On Tuesday, Beckett arrived with a ruler, a calculator, and a bag of pretzels shaped like tiny steering wheels."},{"p":"“They’re for navigation,” he announced."},{"p":"“They’re for eating,” Imani said."},{"p":"“A navigator requires fuel.”"},{"p":"Rafe picked up three. “I’m going to need a lot of fuel.”"},{"p":"Mara had taped a long strip of brown paper across the classroom floor. Blue lines represented rivers. Gray strips were roads. A green square stood for a park, though Beckett insisted it looked like moldy cheese."},{"p":"“Today,” Mara said, “we’re going to find out what happens when the thing you expect to see isn’t where you expect it.”"},{"p":"“Does it explode?” Rafe asked."},{"p":"“Only if you do something spectacularly wrong with the tape dispenser.”"},{"p":"She handed us four route cards. Each card gave us a starting point, a destination, and three checkpoints. We had to walk the paper route without stepping outside a narrow path marked in pencil. At each checkpoint we were supposed to identify two features that confirmed our position."},{"p":"It sounded easy. I had been practicing chart symbols at home, and I could identify a railroad line faster than Dad could find his reading glasses, which wasn’t saying much."},{"p":"I went first."},{"p":"The starting airport was a paper rectangle at the end of the room. I took three steps along the route, found a blue river, and pointed at the little green square beside it."},{"p":"“Checkpoint one. Park by the river.”"},{"p":"“What else?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“There’s a road crossing north of it.”"},{"p":"“Good. Keep going.”"},{"p":"I followed the line around Beckett’s backpack, which he claimed was a mountain range. At the second checkpoint I found the bridge and a triangular patch of woods. I marked my card."},{"p":"Then I reached the third checkpoint and stopped."},{"p":"The green square was missing."},{"p":"I looked up. Imani was standing by the window with her arms folded. Rafe was trying to balance a pretzel on his nose."},{"p":"“Somebody stole the park,” I said."},{"p":"“That’s a serious accusation,” Beckett replied."},{"p":"“It’s supposed to be here.”"},{"p":"Mara didn’t answer."},{"p":"I scanned the floor. There was a green square farther ahead, close to a road that curved sharply left. Maybe the route card had been printed wrong. Or maybe Mara had moved it to see whether we’d notice."},{"p":"“There,” I said, pointing. “I found it.”"},{"p":"“What confirms that position?” she asked."},{"p":"“The park.”"},{"p":"“And?”"},{"p":"I looked for the second clue. The river was gone. So was the railroad line that should have crossed it."},{"p":"“I don’t have another clue.”"},{"p":"“Then what do you know?”"},{"p":"I hated that question. Not because it was hard, but because the answer was small."},{"p":"“I know I haven’t confirmed where I am.”"},{"p":"“Exactly.”"},{"p":"She crouched and lifted a corner of the brown paper. The missing green square was stuck underneath, folded out of sight when she’d unrolled the route."},{"p":"“Your checkpoint didn’t move,” she said. “Your view of it changed.”"},{"p":"Beckett looked personally offended. “That’s cheating.”"},{"p":"“Would a real landmark ever be hidden by a cloud, a building, or the wing?”"},{"p":"“Fine,” he said. “Educational cheating.”"},{"p":"We traded route cards. When Imani took her turn, she didn’t rush. She checked the blue river against the road intersection, counted the turns, and stopped before the missing square without looking up for help."},{"p":"“The expected landmark isn’t visible,” she said. “But the river bends right, and the railroad crosses it here. I think I’m at checkpoint three.”"},{"p":"Mara nodded. “That’s a better-supported conclusion.”"},{"p":"Imani glanced at me. She didn’t smile, which somehow made it worse."},{"p":"At lunch I opened my notebook to a page I’d labeled NAVIGATION TRICKS. Underneath, I’d written CHECK MORE THAN ONE THING in capital letters."},{"p":"Beckett leaned over. “You forgot ‘beware of criminal parks.’”"},{"p":"“I’m trying to learn.”"},{"p":"“So am I. I learned construction paper is untrustworthy.”"},{"p":"I tore a small corner off my sandwich wrapper and drew two arrows on it. One arrow pointed toward a made-up lake. The other pointed along a road."},{"p":"“Okay,” I said. “You’re flying toward this lake. The wind pushes you to the right. You look down and see a road. Which road is it?”"},{"p":"“The one below me,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“There are three roads.”"},{"p":"“Then I’m lost and need a snack.”"},{"p":"Imani reached over and turned the paper sideways. “You compare what you’re seeing with what you expected. And you don’t assume the first road is the right one.”"},{"p":"“What if none of them match?” Rafe asked."},{"p":"I was about to say that I’d keep flying until something looked familiar. It sounded brave in my head. Then I remembered standing over the brown paper, pointing at the wrong patch of green."},{"p":"“You tell Mara you’re not sure,” I said. “And you use the tools and help you have instead of guessing.”"},{"p":"Rafe nodded. “Good. Because my plan was to land at the nearest pretzel factory.”"},{"p":"After lunch, Mara gathered us around a sectional chart. She showed us how a pilot could estimate a position by comparing roads, shorelines, towns, and other features with the chart, while keeping track of time and distance. She also pointed out that a landmark that looked obvious from the classroom might be much harder to recognize from an airplane."},{"p":"“And if you’re unsure of your position during a real flight,” she said, “you keep flying the airplane first. You don’t put your head down and spend the next five minutes wrestling a map.”"},{"p":"I pictured myself doing exactly that, with the chart unfolding across the yoke like an enormous paper bat."},{"p":"“Also,” she added, looking at Rafe, “you don’t search for a pretzel factory.”"},{"p":"“That was one suggestion.”"},{"p":"On the ride home, Dad asked why I had a piece of green paper stuck to my shoe."},{"p":"“A park attacked me.”"},{"p":"“Is that common in aviation?”"},{"p":"“More than you’d think.”"},{"p":"He pulled into our driveway. I watched the mailbox slide past the window, followed by the cracked corner of our sidewalk and the maple tree I’d climbed when I was little. Three things I knew so well I barely noticed them."},{"p":"From the air, I wondered whether I would recognize any of them."},{"p":"Maybe not."},{"p":"I took the green square off my shoe and tucked it into my notebook anyway."}]},{"t":"Chapter Four — The Detour Nobody Voted For","blocks":[{"p":"The morning we left for Ohio, Mom found a jar of pickles in the glove compartment."},{"p":"“Why?” she asked."},{"p":"Dad was checking the tire pressure. He didn’t look up. “Road food.”"},{"p":"“We have sandwiches.”"},{"p":"“Sandwiches don’t crunch like pickles.”"},{"p":"“Neither does a car seat when it’s covered in pickle juice,” she said."},{"p":"I stood beside the open trunk holding the printed route. Mom had packed a cooler, Dad had packed three bags of books he claimed he might read, and I had packed a notebook, binoculars, and two pencils. We were visiting Mom’s cousin outside Dayton, then spending a day at the National Museum of the United States Air Force. I’d been looking forward to the museum for months. Just thinking about all those aircraft made me want to start walking."},{"p":"“Are we ready?” I asked."},{"p":"“We’re ready when the pickles are in the cooler,” Mom said."},{"p":"Dad sighed. “Democracy has failed.”"},{"p":"We got on the road twenty minutes later. I sat in the back with the map spread across my knees, following our route through Virginia. Dad had a navigation app on the dashboard. Mom had a small spiral notebook listing our planned stops, hotel details, and the phone number of the cousin we were visiting."},{"p":"“That’s three ways to navigate,” I said."},{"p":"“Four,” Mom replied. “Your father also believes he can find Ohio by instinct.”"},{"p":"“It’s northwest,” Dad said."},{"p":"“An impressive start.”"},{"p":"For the first hour, everything worked. The road signs matched the route. The weather was bright and warm. We passed fields and neighborhoods, then long stretches of trees with gaps that revealed hills in the distance."},{"p":"I tried predicting when the next interchange would appear. Sometimes I got it right. Other times I discovered I’d been watching the wrong exit number and had to move my finger backward along the map."},{"p":"“You know you don’t have to navigate the whole trip,” Mom said."},{"p":"“I want to.”"},{"p":"“Then keep track of where we are, not just where we’re going.”"},{"p":"It sounded like something Mara would say. I almost told Mom that, but she would have enjoyed it too much."},{"p":"A little after lunch, traffic slowed to a crawl. Red brake lights stretched around a bend and disappeared behind a wooded slope."},{"p":"Dad turned down the radio. “That’s not good.”"},{"p":"The navigation app flashed a warning about a delay ahead and suggested a different route. The blue line jumped off the highway and wandered through a series of smaller roads."},{"p":"“Take it,” I said immediately."},{"p":"“You haven’t looked at it,” Mom said."},{"p":"“It’s shorter.”"},{"p":"“Shorter in distance or shorter in time?”"},{"p":"I checked the screen. “It says we’d save twenty minutes.”"},{"p":"“Maybe,” Dad said. “What kind of roads?”"},{"p":"I zoomed in. The alternate route curved through a town, crossed a river, and rejoined the highway beyond the traffic. On the paper map, I could barely find it. Some of the smaller roads weren’t labeled clearly."},{"p":"“We should trust the app,” I said."},{"p":"Dad glanced at me in the mirror. “Why?”"},{"p":"“Because it knows about the traffic.”"},{"p":"“It knows something about the traffic,” Mom said. “What else should we check?”"},{"p":"I studied the screen. The alternate route included a bridge. A little orange symbol appeared near it. I tapped the symbol and read the warning."},{"p":"“Roadwork. Possible lane closure.”"},{"p":"“That’s useful,” Dad said."},{"p":"I leaned back. Twenty minutes suddenly seemed less impressive."},{"p":"“Could we just stay here?” I asked."},{"p":"“We can,” Mom said. “We have fuel, water, and no appointment until this evening.”"},{"p":"Dad held up the jar of pickles, which had somehow returned to the cup holder. “And emergency provisions.”"},{"p":"“Put those away,” Mom said."},{"p":"We stayed on the highway. Traffic moved slowly for another fifteen minutes, then began to clear. When we reached the cause, I saw orange cones around a truck stopped on the shoulder. A crew was clearing debris, and drivers were merging carefully past the work area."},{"p":"“So the app wasn’t wrong,” I said."},{"p":"“No,” Dad replied. “It gave us an option. We decided not to use it.”"},{"p":"I looked at the paper map again. The route through town was still there, a thin line bending away from the highway. It hadn’t become a bad route just because we’d chosen another one. But it hadn’t become a good route just because the app colored it blue, either."},{"p":"Later, we stopped at a rest area near the mountains. The air smelled like hot pavement and pine needles. Mom went inside to refill our water bottles. Dad walked around the car stretching his back and making noises that suggested his knees were full of gravel."},{"p":"I sat on a low wall and opened my notebook."},{"p":"There was a text from Imani."},{"p":"*How far have you gotten?*"},{"p":"I sent her a picture of the mountains beyond the parking lot."},{"p":"*Far enough to see those.*"},{"p":"She replied with a photograph of Beckett’s latest classroom model. He’d built a paper airplane with three enormous fins and a note on the side reading EXPERIMENTAL, DO NOT MOCK."},{"p":"Rafe had written TOO LATE underneath."},{"p":"I laughed. Then I told Imani about the traffic and the alternate route."},{"p":"*You didn’t take the shortcut?* she wrote."},{"p":"*No. Roadwork on the bridge.*"},{"p":"*Good catch.*"},{"p":"I stared at those two words for a second. A year earlier I might have wanted her to say something bigger. Brilliant. Genius. Future airline captain saves family from disaster. But we’d only made a small decision on an ordinary drive. It was enough that we’d checked."},{"p":"Dad sat beside me with a paper cup of coffee."},{"p":"“You look pleased.”"},{"p":"“We didn’t get lost.”"},{"p":"“That’s a low bar for a vacation.”"},{"p":"“We also didn’t take a route we hadn’t checked.”"},{"p":"He nodded toward the mountains. “Still a long way to go.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“Good. Keep your map handy.”"},{"p":"When we started driving again, I left the paper map open to the next section instead of tracing our entire route to Dayton. The hills grew taller. The highway bent between them, disappearing around corners and appearing again farther up the valley."},{"p":"I couldn’t see Ohio yet."},{"p":"For the first time that morning, I wasn’t in a hurry to pretend I could."}]},{"t":"Chapter Five — The Airplanes That Stayed","blocks":[{"p":"By the time we reached Dayton, the car door had left a permanent impression on my left knee."},{"p":"Dad called it a successful drive because nobody had thrown up. Mom called it a successful drive because we’d arrived before the restaurant closed. I called it a success because I’d spotted the correct exit. I left out the part where I’d almost sent us past it twenty minutes earlier."},{"p":"Our hotel room had two beds, one chair, and a view of a parking garage. Dad admired the concrete as if it were a famous monument."},{"p":"“Magnificent,” he said. “You can almost see the other parking garage.”"},{"p":"Mom handed me a room key. “Breakfast at seven. Museum tomorrow. Shoes off the bed.”"},{"p":"I was already looking up photographs of the National Museum of the United States Air Force."},{"p":"“Lincoln,” she said."},{"p":"I lifted my shoes off the bed."},{"p":"“Thank you.”"},{"p":"The next morning, the museum was so enormous that I stopped walking before we’d even reached the first gallery. Aircraft hung above other aircraft. Wings stretched across the ceiling. A polished floor reflected the bellies of machines that had flown long before my parents were born."},{"p":"Dad nearly walked into me."},{"p":"“Traffic jam,” he said."},{"p":"“Look at that.”"},{"p":"“I am. You’re standing in front of it.”"},{"p":"I moved, and he followed my gaze to a biplane suspended overhead. Its wires and struts made it look fragile beside the metal aircraft beyond it. I could see the difference between the machines, but not the years of wrong guesses and broken parts that separated them."},{"p":"Mom unfolded the museum map. “We have all day, but we cannot see everything at once.”"},{"p":"“We could try,” I said."},{"p":"“That’s what you said about the highway.”"},{"p":"We began in the early-flight exhibits. I stood close enough to a display about the first aviators to read the smaller labels, then stepped back to see how the wings fit together. A museum volunteer pointed out that early pilots had to learn not only how to fly, but how to figure out which ideas worked at all."},{"p":"“Imagine building the thing you intend to test,” Dad said."},{"p":"“Beckett would love that.”"},{"p":"“Beckett would probably add three fins and call it research.”"},{"p":"I took a picture of an exposed engine and sent it to the group chat."},{"p":"**BECKETT:** finally a reasonable amount of parts"},{"p":"**RAFE:** does it come with a cup holder?"},{"p":"**IMANI:** please do not touch anything"},{"p":"I slipped the phone into my pocket before a museum guard could mistake our conversation for a plan."},{"p":"We moved into the World War II galleries, where the aircraft seemed to grow larger every few steps. Under one bomber, Dad read a plaque aloud about crews navigating over long distances without the kind of moving-map screens I’d grown used to seeing in the Cessna."},{"p":"“They had charts and instruments,” I said."},{"p":"“And training,” Dad replied. “And people whose job it was to know where they were.”"},{"p":"I studied the navigator’s station shown in a photograph. It looked cramped and busy, with a small work surface and instruments close together. I imagined trying to draw a line on a chart while an aircraft vibrated around me."},{"p":"“What if they lost their position?” I asked."},{"p":"“Then finding it again mattered a lot,” Dad said. “But let’s read what this display actually says. I don’t want to invent a heroic speech.”"},{"p":"He leaned closer to the panel. I did too."},{"p":"For once, we were both willing to say we didn’t know."},{"p":"Later, in the Cold War galleries, we found the sleek, black SR-71. I had seen one at the Udvar-Hazy Center the year before, but it still seemed impossible that anything shaped like that could leave the ground. The pointed nose, dark skin, and enormous engines made nearby visitors stop talking in the middle of sentences."},{"p":"“You met its cousin,” Mom said."},{"p":"“Not its cousin. Another SR-71.”"},{"p":"“Fine. Its identical very fast friend.”"},{"p":"Dad checked the museum map again. “We need a checkpoint. Lunch?”"},{"p":"“One more exhibit.”"},{"p":"“You said that four exhibits ago.”"},{"p":"My stomach answered with a noise loud enough to make a little kid turn around."},{"p":"We found a table in the museum café. I unwrapped a sandwich while Dad examined the photographs on his phone. He had taken fourteen pictures of landing gear, nine of aircraft noses, and one accidental close-up of his own thumb."},{"p":"“Your thumb’s going in the family album,” Mom told him."},{"p":"“It was an important thumb.”"},{"p":"I showed them a photo of a navigation display. “At the academy, Mara says a route isn’t just a line. You have to know where you are along it.”"},{"p":"“That’s useful for museums, too,” Mom said. “Where are we now?”"},{"p":"I checked the map and pointed confidently to a dining symbol."},{"p":"She rotated the paper ninety degrees."},{"p":"My finger was on the restroom."},{"p":"Dad looked at my sandwich. “Well, at least you’ve found supplies.”"},{"p":"I laughed, even though I’d wanted to be right. The map was printed with north at the top. I’d held it sideways because the corridor outside had turned, and I’d turned the map without noticing."},{"p":"“I knew which building we were in,” I said."},{"p":"“A promising beginning,” Mom replied."},{"p":"After lunch, we followed the signs into another gallery. A little girl in a yellow sweatshirt was arguing with her grandfather beside a large museum map."},{"p":"“We already went there,” she insisted, pointing at a hangar-shaped rectangle."},{"p":"“I don’t think we did,” he said."},{"p":"She looked close to tears. “But that’s where the airplane with the teeth was.”"},{"p":"I recognized the description. We’d passed an aircraft with painted nose art earlier. I almost told them exactly where to go. Then I noticed the girl was pointing at a photograph rather than a location."},{"p":"“I think I saw that one,” I said. “But I might be mixing up the galleries. Want to check the exhibit names with me?”"},{"p":"She stared at me as though I’d asked whether she wanted homework."},{"p":"Her grandfather smiled. “We’d appreciate it.”"},{"p":"We matched the photograph to a label, found the correct gallery on the map, and traced the route from the sign above our heads. The girl followed the line with her finger."},{"p":"“So we go past the big silver one?”"},{"p":"“That’s what the map says,” I told her. “And we can check the next sign.”"},{"p":"She ran ahead three steps, then returned to take her grandfather’s hand."},{"p":"I watched them go."},{"p":"“That was nice,” Mom said."},{"p":"“I nearly sent them the wrong way.”"},{"p":"“But you didn’t.”"},{"p":"I looked down at the map, now folded so the corridor we needed was visible. It had taken longer to check than to guess. We had time."},{"p":"At the end of the day, my feet hurt, my camera roll was ridiculous, and there were still exhibits we hadn’t seen. Dad offered to come back another year."},{"p":"“We didn’t finish,” I said."},{"p":"“Museums aren’t video games,” he replied. “You don’t have to beat them.”"},{"p":"Outside, the afternoon light was bright enough to make me squint. Airplanes had been flying for more than a century, and the museum had shown me only pieces of the story. I couldn’t have explained every instrument I’d seen. I couldn’t even remember every aircraft name."},{"p":"But I knew how to find the entrance again."},{"p":"That felt like a useful place to start."}]},{"t":"Chapter Six — The Pencil Mark","blocks":[{"p":"The first thing Beckett said when I returned to the academy was, “Did you bring me a jet engine?”"},{"p":"“It wouldn’t fit in the trunk.”"},{"p":"“Excuses.”"},{"p":"He was sitting at a table with a folded sectional chart and a ruler so long it stuck out past his elbow. Rafe had placed a tiny plastic dinosaur beside the chart’s legend."},{"p":"“What’s that for?” I asked."},{"p":"“Emergency diversion,” Rafe said. “The dinosaur has declared this airport unsuitable.”"},{"p":"“Because of what?” I asked."},{"p":"“Other dinosaurs.”"},{"p":"Imani looked up from her notebook. “Mara asked us to identify checkpoints. Rafe identified a prehistoric incident.”"},{"p":"“It’s called thinking ahead.”"},{"p":"I put my museum postcard on the table. Beckett picked it up, inspected the aircraft on the front, and set it beside the dinosaur."},{"p":"“Better,” he said. “Still not an engine.”"},{"p":"Mara entered carrying a stack of worksheets and a clear plastic plotting tool. She set the papers down and pointed to a route already marked on the chart."},{"p":"“Today, nobody gets to tell me where they want to be,” she said. “I want to know how they’ll tell where they actually are.”"},{"p":"The route was a classroom exercise, not a flight plan we would use in the air. It began at an airport, crossed a river, passed a town, and continued toward another airport. Three checkpoints were marked with small circles."},{"p":"“Work in pairs,” Mara said. “Describe how you’d recognize each one from the air. Include something that could prove your first guess wrong.”"},{"p":"Beckett and I ended up together. Neither of us complained. A new academy record."},{"p":"Our first checkpoint was a highway interchange beside a river. On the chart, it looked obvious. Two roads met, one curved around the other, and a bridge crossed the water just east of them."},{"p":"“Easy,” I said. “Find the bridge.”"},{"p":"Beckett traced the river upstream with his ruler. “There are two bridges.”"},{"p":"“The interchange is beside the first one.”"},{"p":"“First from which direction?”"},{"p":"I pointed to the line we’d drawn. “From where we’re coming.”"},{"p":"“What if we drift off course before we get there?”"},{"p":"I leaned closer. He had a point. The second bridge was farther from the interchange, but a nearby road curved in a similar way."},{"p":"“We’d check the shape of the interchange,” I said."},{"p":"“And the town on the west side,” Beckett added. “If we can’t find both, maybe it’s not the checkpoint.”"},{"p":"I wrote that down."},{"p":"For the second checkpoint, we were supposed to recognize a reservoir. It had a narrow northern end and a wider basin to the south. On the worksheet’s aerial photograph, though, the water level was lower than on the chart. The shoreline looked different."},{"p":"“That’s unfair,” I said."},{"p":"“Water does that,” Beckett replied."},{"p":"“I know water levels change.”"},{"p":"“Then why are you mad at water?”"},{"p":"I pressed harder with my pencil. The point snapped."},{"p":"“Because water doesn’t have to take this quiz.”"},{"p":"Across the table, Rafe snorted so hard he dropped his dinosaur."},{"p":"Mara came over while I sharpened my pencil."},{"p":"“What’s the disagreement?”"},{"p":"“The reservoir doesn’t look like the chart,” I said."},{"p":"“What else could you use?”"},{"p":"Beckett pointed to a highway running along the east side and a small town south of the water. “Those.”"},{"p":"“Good. What would you do if you couldn’t identify the reservoir confidently?”"},{"p":"I started to say we’d look harder. Then I pictured an airplane flying farther off course while everyone aboard argued about a lake."},{"p":"“We’d tell you,” I said. “And use other information to work out where we were.”"},{"p":"“That’s the start of an answer,” Mara replied. “You’d maintain safe control of the airplane, use available navigation information, and ask for help as appropriate. You wouldn’t keep flying a guess because it was written in pencil.”"},{"p":"She tapped the chart. “This exercise is about recognizing uncertainty early. Not about winning a staring contest with a reservoir.”"},{"p":"When she moved away, Beckett grinned."},{"p":"“I think the reservoir won.”"},{"p":"“It cheated.”"},{"p":"We finished the third checkpoint without an argument, which was less impressive than it sounded because it was a town with a distinctive airport nearby. Then Mara handed each pair a second sheet."},{"p":"“New information,” she announced. “Your first checkpoint is hidden beneath a cloud shadow. The reservoir is harder to identify than expected. Your fictional pilot reports being uncertain of position. What happens next?”"},{"p":"Rafe raised his hand. “Does the dinosaur survive?”"},{"p":"“The dinosaur is not part of the approved navigation equipment,” Mara said."},{"p":"“That’s what they said before the asteroid.”"},{"p":"I glanced at Beckett. He was still smiling, but he had already pulled the chart closer."},{"p":"We worked through the options. A properly functioning GPS could help us check our position. Other landmarks might confirm it, and air traffic control could sometimes assist. We also had to consider fuel and weather. None of that required pretending our first guess was right."},{"p":"“Would you turn around?” Beckett asked me."},{"p":"“Maybe. Depends on the situation.”"},{"p":"“That’s not an answer.”"},{"p":"“It’s a better answer than always turning around.”"},{"p":"He considered that. “Fair.”"},{"p":"I drew a small question mark beside the reservoir instead of crossing it out. It was still a useful feature. It just wasn’t proof all by itself."},{"p":"After class, we walked out toward the fence where we sometimes watched aircraft taxi past. The afternoon was warm, and somebody nearby had cut the grass. A Cessna moved slowly along a taxiway with its landing light on."},{"p":"Imani joined us, carrying her notebook under one arm."},{"p":"“How was Dayton?” she asked."},{"p":"“Huge. We got lost inside the museum.”"},{"p":"“Of course you did.”"},{"p":"“Only once. Maybe twice.”"},{"p":"“How many times did your dad photograph his thumb?”"},{"p":"“One confirmed sighting.”"},{"p":"She laughed. It was good to hear her laugh without wondering whether I’d said the wrong thing."},{"p":"“My grandmother liked the picture of the old airplane,” she said. “The one with all the wires.”"},{"p":"“I can send her more, if she wants.”"},{"p":"“She said one was enough.”"},{"p":"“Reasonable. My phone has eighty-seven.”"},{"p":"Imani shook her head. “You have a problem.”"},{"p":"“I have a collection.”"},{"p":"She walked ahead to catch Rafe, who was trying to balance the plastic dinosaur on a fence post. Beckett stayed beside me."},{"p":"“You know,” he said, “you could’ve just guessed on the reservoir question. Mara might not have noticed.”"},{"p":"“You would’ve noticed.”"},{"p":"“Definitely.”"},{"p":"“Then you’d have reminded me forever.”"},{"p":"“Also definitely.”"},{"p":"We watched the Cessna turn onto the runway and pause. The pilot waited while another airplane passed overhead in the traffic pattern. I couldn’t hear the radio calls from where we stood. I couldn’t know what was being said inside either cockpit."},{"p":"The waiting didn’t bother me."},{"p":"“Hey,” Beckett said, pointing toward the sky. “What kind of airplane is that one?”"},{"p":"A small aircraft was moving beyond the runway, too far away for me to make out much besides its high wing and pale fuselage."},{"p":"I nearly guessed a model anyway."},{"p":"“Not sure,” I said. “We’d need a better look.”"},{"p":"Beckett gave me an exaggerated expression of horror."},{"p":"“Lincoln Burgess admits uncertainty. Somebody write down the date.”"},{"p":"“I’ve got a pencil,” I said."},{"p":"He held out the worksheet. I wrote the date in the corner, right beside the little question mark."},{"p":"Then we both looked up again."}]},{"t":"Chapter Seven — The Highway That Wasn’t There","blocks":[{"p":"On Tuesday, Mara stretched blue painter’s tape across the classroom floor and declared it a river."},{"p":"Rafe stared at it. “That’s the saddest river I’ve ever seen.”"},{"p":"“It has an excellent safety record,” Mara said."},{"p":"She set a folding chair beside the tape, placed a paper cup upside down on the chair, and handed Beckett a card with a drawing of a bridge."},{"p":"“Your bridge,” she told him."},{"p":"“Finally. Infrastructure.”"},{"p":"“You are the only person I’ve met who says that like it’s a birthday present,” Imani said."},{"p":"The room smelled like dry-erase markers and somebody’s cinnamon gum. Outside, a mower bumped along the edge of the ramp. I had my navigation notebook open, but I’d stopped drawing neat little airplanes in the margins after Mara caught me giving one of them teeth."},{"p":"“Today,” she said, “we’re going to talk about what happens when a plan stops matching the view.”"},{"p":"That sounded suspiciously like a description of my family’s trip to Ohio."},{"p":"She spread a sectional chart across two tables and put a clear ruler over it. The route was imaginary; no one was taking off to find these checkpoints. We were supposed to identify checkpoints, estimate distances, and decide how we’d recognize when we’d reached them."},{"p":"“There’s a highway beside this river,” Mara said. “Your plan says you’ll follow it until you see a bridge. What if the highway is closed and the bridge has been removed?”"},{"p":"“Did somebody steal it?” Rafe asked."},{"p":"“Construction.”"},{"p":"“Less interesting.”"},{"p":"“Still inconvenient,” Mara said."},{"p":"I looked at the chart. “If the bridge isn’t there, the chart’s wrong.”"},{"p":"“Or out of date,” Imani said."},{"p":"“That’s what I meant.”"},{"p":"“It is not what you said.”"},{"p":"I made a face at her. She made one back, which I counted as a reasonable exchange between friends."},{"p":"Mara pointed to the publication information printed on the chart. “Aeronautical charts have effective dates. Pilots use current information, and they don’t assume that every feature on the ground will remain unchanged forever. Roads are rebuilt. Towers appear. Other landmarks disappear.”"},{"p":"Beckett held up his bridge card. “I want a refund.”"},{"p":"“You paid nothing,” Mara said."},{"p":"“Exactly the level of service I received.”"},{"p":"She moved the card out of sight behind a folder. “Now what?”"},{"p":"I reached for the ruler. “Keep going until the next town.”"},{"p":"“How do you know it’s the next town?” Mara asked."},{"p":"I opened my mouth, then closed it."},{"p":"The town had a name on the chart, but a name wasn’t painted across its rooftops. I checked the railroad line, the bend in the river, and an airport marked farther east."},{"p":"“I’d want more than one feature,” I said. “And I’d need to know my position before deciding what direction to go.”"},{"p":"Mara nodded. “Good. If you’re unsure of your position in an actual airplane, don’t just keep flying and hope the next thing looks familiar. Maintain control, check what you know, use available navigation equipment correctly, and ask for help when appropriate.”"},{"p":"“What if the GPS says we’re right?” Beckett asked."},{"p":"“Then we consider that information. We don’t stop thinking. GPS is useful, but pilots still need to interpret it correctly, check the setup, and know what to do if it becomes unavailable.”"},{"p":"She gave us a few minutes to compare possible landmarks. Imani found a highway interchange that would be easy to mistake for another one. Rafe located a lake shaped, according to him, like a potato wearing a hat."},{"p":"“That is a peninsula,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Potato peninsula.”"},{"p":"“It’s not even the right lake.”"},{"p":"“Then the potato is lost.”"},{"p":"I tried not to laugh while measuring a distance. The ruler slipped. Back to zero."},{"p":"When Mara checked our work, she didn’t tell us the best answer right away. She asked each pair what information would make us change our minds. Beckett wanted a second recognizable landmark. I wanted to check the route against the GPS position in the scenario. Imani asked about the weather, because a landmark beneath a low cloud layer might not be visible even if it was exactly where the chart said."},{"p":"“You are all thinking about the same problem from different directions,” Mara said."},{"p":"“Mine has a dinosaur,” Rafe said."},{"p":"“Yours has a dinosaur on the worksheet,” Mara corrected. “Not in the airplane.”"},{"p":"After class, I was still annoyed about the bridge. Not because I thought my answer had been brilliant, but because I knew how easily I’d have kept following the imaginary highway just to avoid admitting I didn’t know where I was."},{"p":"Dad picked me up in the library’s old sedan, which smelled like paperback books and peppermint. He had a cardboard box of returned novels on the back seat."},{"p":"“How was navigation?” he asked."},{"p":"“A bridge vanished.”"},{"p":"He glanced at me. “Should I call somebody?”"},{"p":"“It was made of paper.”"},{"p":"“That explains the lack of news coverage.”"},{"p":"At a stoplight, he noticed the ruler sticking out of my notebook. “You still working on the trip route?”"},{"p":"“Not the driving route. I was looking at how you’d plan an airplane route.”"},{"p":"“Any easier?”"},{"p":"“More complicated.”"},{"p":"“Sounds right.”"},{"p":"I watched the traffic light turn green. A delivery truck moved into the intersection ahead of us, then stopped because a car had blocked the far lane. Dad waited, though the light was still green."},{"p":"“You’re allowed to go,” I said."},{"p":"“I’m allowed to enter when I can clear the intersection. Not the same thing.”"},{"p":"I looked down at the chart in my lap. I’d been treating the drawn line as a promise. But the line didn’t know about a missing bridge, a cloud, a tired pilot, or a road full of cars that had nowhere to go."},{"p":"“Dad?”"},{"p":"“Yeah?”"},{"p":"“When we were driving to Dayton, did you know the detour wouldn’t work before I showed it to you?”"},{"p":"“Nope. I checked because you were excited about it, and I wanted to see whether it was worth trying.”"},{"p":"“You could’ve just told me it was a bad idea.”"},{"p":"“Would you have believed me?”"},{"p":"“Probably not.”"},{"p":"“There you go.”"},{"p":"The truck finally cleared the intersection. Dad eased forward. I drew a little box around the bridge on my worksheet and wrote, CHECK AGAIN, in letters big enough that even Rafe’s dinosaur could read them."}]},{"t":"Chapter Eight — A Dot on the Windshield","blocks":[{"p":"The airplane looked smaller than I remembered."},{"p":"That always happened after I spent too long staring at airliners and museum aircraft. The Cessna 172S wasn’t a fighter jet. It had four seats, a high wing, and a door that required a particular shove before it latched. It also had a habit of reminding me that knowing something on paper didn’t make me good at doing it."},{"p":"Mara met me at the airplane with a clipboard tucked under her arm."},{"p":"“What’s the plan today?” she asked."},{"p":"“Navigation practice. Visual checkpoints. Staying on course.”"},{"p":"“And if the checkpoints aren’t where you expect?”"},{"p":"“Check my position. Don’t invent a new geography.”"},{"p":"“Excellent. Virginia appreciates that.”"},{"p":"We completed the preflight together. I read the checklist and looked where it told me to look, not just where I’d looked last time. Mara checked my work. I paused at a fuel cap, and Mara checked that it was properly secured before we moved on. By the time we were strapped in, my shirt was sticking to my back."},{"p":"The engine started with a rough cough that settled into its familiar rumble. We used the checklist for the before-taxi items. Mara made the radio calls while I watched the taxiway and tried not to steer with my entire leg."},{"p":"At the run-up area, we performed the required checks and discussed our plan again. Mara would remain the pilot in command throughout. The route was a short, instructor-supervised training exercise in suitable weather, with an easy return available. We weren’t pretending I was ready to go wandering across three states by myself."},{"p":"“You have two checkpoints,” she said. “A road crossing and a broad bend in a waterway. We’ll use those, and we’ll compare them with what the GPS shows. I want you to tell me what you’re confident about and what you’re only guessing.”"},{"p":"“What if I guess wrong?”"},{"p":"“Then we find out. That’s why we’re here.”"},{"p":"Mara directed the departure and climb. The runway dropped away beneath us. From above, the airport became a pattern of pale pavement, hangars, and patches of green. The roads beyond it looked like somebody had spilled spaghetti across a board game."},{"p":"When Mara offered me the controls, I said, “My airplane,” the way she’d taught me."},{"p":"“Your airplane.”"},{"p":"I held a heading using a point on the horizon, with quick instrument checks to confirm what the airplane was doing. It took effort not to stare at the heading indicator. Those little numbers were tempting. They looked certain."},{"p":"“Where’s your first checkpoint?” Mara asked."},{"p":"I searched ahead. “Somewhere near that cluster of buildings.”"},{"p":"“Somewhere is a large place.”"},{"p":"“The road crossing should be just beyond them.”"},{"p":"“Should be. Can you see it?”"},{"p":"I leaned forward against the shoulder harness. “Not yet.”"},{"p":"“Good answer. Keep flying the airplane.”"},{"p":"A faint haze softened the far distance, though the visibility was good for our lesson. The ground seemed to move slowly beneath us. Then I noticed a road running diagonally toward another road, but the intersection was hidden behind a patch of trees."},{"p":"“There,” I said. “I think.”"},{"p":"“What supports that?”"},{"p":"“The buildings. The direction of the road. And the waterway is farther beyond it.”"},{"p":"“What doesn’t support it?”"},{"p":"That was harder."},{"p":"“I can’t actually see the crossing.”"},{"p":"“Exactly.”"},{"p":"She had me compare our position with the GPS display, explaining what we were looking at rather than letting me chase the little airplane symbol. The display put us in the general area I’d identified. It still didn’t mean I had seen the hidden intersection."},{"p":"“So do I count it?” I asked."},{"p":"“Tell me what you know.”"},{"p":"“We’re in the right area. I have not positively identified the crossing.”"},{"p":"“Then don’t check it off yet.”"},{"p":"I wanted to argue. We were close. I was pretty sure. And there was a ridiculous part of my brain that believed a worksheet with two checked boxes would somehow make the flight more successful."},{"p":"But I left the box empty."},{"p":"A few minutes later, the waterway came into view. Its broad curve matched the chart, and a narrow road ran alongside it. I pointed out the features one at a time."},{"p":"“That’s the bend,” I said. “I can see the road and the shape of the shoreline.”"},{"p":"“I agree. How’s your altitude?”"},{"p":"I checked. I’d let it wander a little while studying the ground."},{"p":"“Low,” I admitted, making a small correction under Mara’s supervision."},{"p":"“That’s why we keep the airplane first in our attention,” she said. “A checkpoint isn’t much use if we stop flying to admire it.”"},{"p":"“The ground is very distracting.”"},{"p":"“It’s been doing that to pilots for a long time.”"},{"p":"We turned toward another practice area. Mara had me identify a town by its position relative to a road and a river. I got it wrong. Not spectacularly wrong. Just wrong enough to feel my ears getting hot."},{"p":"“I thought that was the town,” I said."},{"p":"“What would help you confirm?”"},{"p":"“A second landmark. And checking our actual position.”"},{"p":"“Go ahead. I’ll take the airplane while you look.”"},{"p":"“Your airplane,” I said, relieved."},{"p":"“My airplane.”"},{"p":"With Mara flying, I unfolded the chart more carefully. I found the road junction I’d missed, then the town we’d actually passed. I had confused two clusters of buildings that looked almost identical from our altitude."},{"p":"“We’re east of where I thought,” I said."},{"p":"“That’s right.”"},{"p":"“I was sure.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"I waited for her to say something else. She didn’t. She adjusted the airplane and let me sit with it for a few seconds."},{"p":"“Can I try the next one?” I asked."},{"p":"“Of course. That’s the lesson.”"},{"p":"We returned toward the airport before I felt finished, which was probably how most lessons would end for the rest of my life. Mara handled the approach and landing, talking me through the important decisions without pretending I was qualified to perform them on my own. I watched for the runway, listened to the radio, and kept my hands clear when she needed them clear."},{"p":"After shutdown, we sat for a moment in the quiet cockpit. The cooling engine ticked beneath the cowling."},{"p":"“What went well?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“I found the river bend. And I didn’t check off the road crossing when I couldn’t see it.”"},{"p":"“What needs work?”"},{"p":"“Everything else?”"},{"p":"She raised an eyebrow."},{"p":"“Okay. I need to keep flying the airplane while I’m looking for things. And I need to confirm my position before I announce that I’ve found a town.”"},{"p":"“Good. Anything else?”"},{"p":"I looked at the two boxes on my worksheet. One was empty. The other had a pencil check beside it."},{"p":"“I think I care too much about finishing the sheet.”"},{"p":"“That’s worth noticing.”"},{"p":"We climbed out into the heat. Across the ramp, Beckett and Rafe were standing with Imani beside a picnic table. Rafe had a bag of chips and was trying to toss them into his own mouth from an impressive distance. Most of them were landing on his shirt."},{"p":"“How’d it go?” Imani called."},{"p":"“I found one thing and misidentified another.”"},{"p":"“So, a normal day?” Beckett said."},{"p":"“A normal day with an airplane.”"},{"p":"Rafe caught a chip and raised both arms. “Witnesses!”"},{"p":"“We witnessed the other eleven,” Imani said."},{"p":"I sat beside them and opened my notebook. The blank box on the worksheet still bothered me a little. I didn’t erase it or fill it in."},{"p":"Beckett leaned over. “You forgot one.”"},{"p":"“No,” I said. “I didn’t see it clearly enough.”"},{"p":"He studied the paper, then nodded once."},{"p":"“Fair.”"},{"p":"I wrote the date across the top and added a note beneath the empty box: NOT CONFIRMED."},{"p":"Rafe looked over my shoulder. “That’s a terrible name for a town.”"},{"p":"“It’s not a town.”"},{"p":"“It could be. Population: nobody knows.”"},{"p":"Imani laughed so hard she nearly knocked the chip bag off the table. I laughed too, and for once I didn’t feel any urge to explain that Rafe’s joke was technically impossible."}]},{"t":"Chapter Nine — The Minutes We Couldn’t Borrow","blocks":[{"p":"The next Tuesday, Dad found me at the kitchen table with a ruler, a calculator, and three different kinds of snack crackers."},{"p":"“Should I ask?” he said."},{"p":"“The round ones are airports. The square ones are checkpoints.”"},{"p":"“And the fish-shaped ones?”"},{"p":"“Those are not part of the system.”"},{"p":"He picked up a fish cracker. “Good. Because one of them just diverted.”"},{"p":"I had been trying to figure out how far an airplane could travel before lunch. Not because I planned to go anywhere by myself. I was fifteen. Mara, not me, made the actual go-or-no-go decisions for our lessons. But a route on a chart looked so simple until you added a clock. Then every line seemed to have an argument with every other line."},{"p":"“If we’re going ninety knots,” I said, “we should cover ninety nautical miles in an hour.”"},{"p":"Dad leaned against the counter. “Is that what you did last summer with the wind?”"},{"p":"“It’s what the airplane would do if ninety knots were our groundspeed. Which it probably isn’t.”"},{"p":"“Why not?”"},{"p":"I stared at him. “You know why not.”"},{"p":"“I do. I’m checking whether you do.”"},{"p":"He had become alarmingly good at that."},{"p":"I slid a square cracker two inches down the table. “The wind changes how fast we move over the ground. Airspeed isn’t the same as groundspeed. And we need the distance in nautical miles, not whatever the map app says in regular miles.”"},{"p":"“I withdraw my objection to the crackers.”"},{"p":"He went to find his glasses, which were already on his head."},{"p":"At the academy on Thursday, Mara had replaced the usual worksheets with four envelopes. Each held a route drawn on a simplified practice chart, a fictional wind forecast, and a small card describing an airplane. None of the cards represented an aircraft we were actually going to dispatch. Mara made that point before Rafe could ask whether we were allowed to fly to the snack machine."},{"p":"“You’re planning a supervised exercise,” she told us. “The goal is to catch bad assumptions on paper, where they’re cheap.”"},{"p":"“How cheap?” Rafe asked."},{"p":"“Free, unless Beckett starts charging for pencil lead.”"},{"p":"Beckett held up a mechanical pencil. “Rates are negotiable.”"},{"p":"My envelope said our first leg was thirty-six nautical miles. We were given an estimated groundspeed of seventy-two knots for that leg. Easy. Half an hour."},{"p":"I wrote 30 MIN in a box and looked at the next leg: forty-eight nautical miles at ninety-six knots. Another thirty minutes. That made an hour of flying between the points on the worksheet."},{"p":"I was feeling excellent about myself until Imani said, “What about the climb?”"},{"p":"“What about it?”"},{"p":"“Our groundspeeds are estimates for the legs. Does the problem say whether they include the climb?”"},{"p":"I reread the card. It did not."},{"p":"“Maybe we’re supposed to ignore it,” I said."},{"p":"“Maybe we’re supposed to ask,” she replied."},{"p":"Mara was walking behind us, checking on another table. I raised my hand."},{"p":"“Good question,” she said when I asked. “For this exercise, assume the leg times already account for the planned climb and descent. In real planning, you would use appropriate aircraft performance information, not a convenient guess.”"},{"p":"I wrote that down. The margin of my worksheet was filling up with things that weren’t answers."},{"p":"Beckett had his own problem. He had calculated a leg using statute miles from a road map, which made his time estimate wrong even though his arithmetic was right. He noticed before Mara did and crossed it out so hard his pencil tore the paper."},{"p":"“You can erase it,” I said."},{"p":"“I am erasing it. Aggressively.”"},{"p":"“You might erase the table.”"},{"p":"“Then I’ll be done early.”"},{"p":"Rafe announced that his route would take “approximately one whole eternity” because he’d divided distance by airspeed without checking the wind information. Mara told him eternity wasn’t an approved unit on the worksheet."},{"p":"We laughed, but the next card stopped us."},{"p":"It described a hypothetical flight with a planned cruise fuel burn, a separate allowance for taxi and climb, and a required reserve that had to remain available at the end. Mara had left out the airplane’s usable fuel quantity."},{"p":"“We can’t finish,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“We can calculate what the trip might require,” I said."},{"p":"“But not whether there’s enough fuel aboard,” Imani added."},{"p":"“Right.” I checked the card again, irritated by the missing number. “She forgot it.”"},{"p":"Mara was standing directly behind my chair."},{"p":"“Did I?”"},{"p":"I felt the back of my neck heat up."},{"p":"“No,” I said. “You probably didn’t.”"},{"p":"“What do you need?”"},{"p":"“The airplane’s usable fuel, how much is actually aboard, and its performance information. We’d also check the conditions and required reserves.”"},{"p":"“Better. And is a planned fuel number the same as what you know is aboard?”"},{"p":"“Not necessarily.”"},{"p":"“Exactly.”"},{"p":"She moved on, leaving the question unanswered."},{"p":"At the end of the exercise, Mara asked us to present our route. I described the two half-hour legs, the missing fuel information, and our decision not to label the fictional trip ready for departure."},{"p":"“You didn’t get a finished answer,” she said."},{"p":"“We got a list of things we’d need before there could be one.”"},{"p":"She nodded and turned to the next group."},{"p":"It should have felt like a win. Instead I kept looking at the blank fuel box. It bothered me the same way the unconfirmed checkpoint had bothered me last week."},{"p":"During the break, Imani caught me trying to straighten the torn edge of Beckett’s worksheet."},{"p":"“You know you don’t have to fix everybody’s paper,” she said."},{"p":"“It’s hanging off the desk.”"},{"p":"“Then it can hang.”"},{"p":"She sat down across from me and opened a bag of pretzels. Her phone buzzed. She glanced at the screen and put it face down without answering."},{"p":"I waited. I had learned that waiting was a real activity, even if it didn’t look like one."},{"p":"“Grandma has another appointment Friday,” she said after a while. “Mom’s taking her.”"},{"p":"“How’s she doing?”"},{"p":"“Some days good. Some days she gets tired before lunch.”"},{"p":"“That sounds hard.”"},{"p":"Imani nodded. She didn’t look at me, but she didn’t move away either."},{"p":"“Do you want a pretzel?” she asked."},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"“Then stop folding that paper before Beckett thinks you’re stealing his homework.”"},{"p":"I took a pretzel. Across the room, Beckett was arguing with Rafe about whether a fictional airport could legally be named Pretzel International. The fuel box stayed empty. Nobody was hurt by it."},{"p":"That evening I told Dad I’d learned something about planning."},{"p":"“How much fuel a plane needs?” he asked."},{"p":"“Sort of. Mostly that I can’t calculate my way around missing information.”"},{"p":"He nodded, then pointed at the empty cracker bowl."},{"p":"“Does that include knowing who ate all the fish?”"},{"p":"“There were witnesses,” I said."},{"p":"“Unreliable witnesses.”"},{"p":"I decided not to tell him I had eaten the last four."}]},{"t":"Chapter Ten — The Airport We Didn’t Choose","blocks":[{"p":"Saturday’s plan was supposed to be simple. Mara would take me on another dual lesson, we’d practice following a short route using visual checkpoints, and I’d help compare our actual times with the estimates we’d made on the ground."},{"p":"The word *simple* should have been my warning."},{"p":"At the airport, the morning was bright and a little hazy. The breeze carried the smell of hot pavement and somebody’s breakfast sandwich from the pilot lounge. Beckett had already claimed the chair nearest the outlet for his tablet. Rafe was drawing a cartoon airplane with what appeared to be six engines and a swimming pool."},{"p":"“That’s not going to fly,” I said."},{"p":"“It’s going to be very popular while not flying,” he said."},{"p":"Imani was bent over a chart with Mara. She gave me a quick wave but didn’t stop what she was doing. Her grandmother’s appointment had been the day before, and I hadn’t asked for an update. I wanted to. I also remembered how badly things had gone the last time I treated her family’s news like something I was entitled to know."},{"p":"Mara called me over."},{"p":"“We’re going to work on a diversion today,” she said."},{"p":"I looked at the planned route on the table. “From where?”"},{"p":"“That’s part of the exercise. We’ll brief the options now, and I’ll give you the change once we’re in the practice area.”"},{"p":"She unfolded a current chart and pointed out two possible airports. We talked about runway length, weather, airspace, fuel, and whether an airport would actually suit the airplane and the situation. She reminded me that identifying a place on a chart wasn’t the same as confirming it was available and appropriate."},{"p":"“What if I pick the wrong one?” I asked."},{"p":"“Then we work through why. This is a lesson, Lincoln. I’m the pilot in command, and the real decisions are mine.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“Knowing doesn’t prevent you from trying to do my job.”"},{"p":"“That’s unfairly accurate.”"},{"p":"Before we went outside, Mara had me calculate a rough time for one leg using the practice groundspeed. I used the distance and checked the units twice. We compared the estimate with the route notes. Then she asked me to say out loud what might make the actual time different."},{"p":"“Wind,” I said. “Changes in the route. Climb and descent. Maybe the estimate was wrong to begin with.”"},{"p":"“Good. Don’t confuse a number you calculated with a promise the airplane made.”"},{"p":"The preflight inspection took longer than I expected. A small smudge on the windscreen made me think there was a crack, and I asked Mara to look. It was only dirt. I felt foolish until she told me to keep asking when something looked wrong."},{"p":"We climbed into the Cessna, fastened our belts, and went through the appropriate checklists together. I listened to Mara’s traffic announcements and watched for other aircraft. Once airborne and clear of the busiest part of the departure, she gave me the controls under her supervision."},{"p":"The first checkpoint was a broad road bending around a patch of trees. I found it quickly. The second took longer. I kept glancing down at the chart, then up at the ground, then at the instruments."},{"p":"“Eyes outside,” Mara reminded me. “I’ll take the airplane if you need more time with the chart.”"},{"p":"“I can do it.”"},{"p":"A few seconds later my altitude had drifted."},{"p":"“Actually, could you take the airplane while I check something?”"},{"p":"“My airplane.”"},{"p":"“Your airplane.”"},{"p":"I located the second checkpoint without having to pretend I’d seen it earlier. It was a small improvement, but it felt useful."},{"p":"Mara gave me the controls again. We were well within the planned training area when she tapped the route sheet."},{"p":"“Here’s your change. Imagine the destination we briefed is no longer suitable. Talk me through an alternate.”"},{"p":"I pointed to the nearest airport symbol on the chart. “That one.”"},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"“It’s close.”"},{"p":"“What else do you know about it?”"},{"p":"I squinted at the chart. Not enough. I knew the location, but I hadn’t reviewed its runway information or checked the latest conditions. I didn’t even know whether its services mattered for our scenario."},{"p":"“Not much,” I admitted."},{"p":"“Try again.”"},{"p":"I felt a familiar urge to produce an answer immediately. In class, a pause was embarrassing. In an airplane, I was beginning to understand, a confident wrong answer could be much worse."},{"p":"“Can you take the airplane while I compare the options?”"},{"p":"“My airplane.”"},{"p":"With Mara flying, I checked the information we’d prepared on the ground. One of our briefed alternatives was farther away, but it had a runway and conditions that better matched the scenario. I talked through the estimated distance and time, then stopped myself before I called it the best choice."},{"p":"“I’d verify the current weather and airport information,” I said. “And make sure we have enough fuel for the revised plan, including reserves.”"},{"p":"“Good. What would you do if you couldn’t establish those things?”"},{"p":"“Ask for help. Or choose an option we can verify.”"},{"p":"She nodded. “That’s the reasoning I wanted to hear. We’re not actually diverting there today.”"},{"p":"I looked up. “We’re not?”"},{"p":"“Our planned lesson remains suitable. This was the decision exercise.”"},{"p":"“I was kind of hoping we’d go somewhere different.”"},{"p":"“That’s why we separate wanting an adventure from needing a diversion.”"},{"p":"We resumed the route, and Mara let me compare the time to our next checkpoint with my estimate. We were a few minutes off. I started to recalculate everything at once and made a mess of the numbers."},{"p":"“One thing at a time,” she said. “First, where are we?”"},{"p":"I found the road and a bend in the waterway. “Here.”"},{"p":"“Then what do you know?”"},{"p":"“The actual time to this point. And the remaining distance.”"},{"p":"“What can you update?”"},{"p":"“Our estimated time to the next checkpoint, using what we’ve observed and the conditions now.”"},{"p":"We worked through it together. Mara kept the airplane where it needed to be while I handled the arithmetic. When I gave her the revised estimate, I said, “About,” before the number."},{"p":"“Why about?” she asked."},{"p":"“Because the wind doesn’t care that I wrote it neatly.”"},{"p":"She laughed. “That’s one way to put it.”"},{"p":"On the way back, a cloud shadow crossed the fields below us. It slid over the roads and rooftops, changing their colors. For a second I lost the landmark I had been following. I wanted to turn the airplane to chase it."},{"p":"Instead I held my assigned heading and told Mara what I’d lost. She took a look, confirmed our position, and helped me identify the next reference. Nothing dramatic happened. I liked that part."},{"p":"Mara flew the approach and landing. After we parked and shut down, I sat with the headset resting in my lap."},{"p":"“What was your first choice for the diversion?” she asked."},{"p":"“The closest airport.”"},{"p":"“And your second?”"},{"p":"“The one I could actually explain.”"},{"p":"“Which matters more?”"},{"p":"“Whether it’s suitable. Not whether it makes me look fast.”"},{"p":"She handed me the route sheet. “Write that in your own words before you forget it.”"},{"p":"I wrote: CLOSEST ISN’T ALWAYS BEST. CHECK FIRST."},{"p":"Back inside, Beckett wanted to know if we’d gone anywhere exciting."},{"p":"“No,” I said. “We stayed on the planned route.”"},{"p":"“Then why do you look so pleased?” he asked."},{"p":"“I made a wrong choice without having to actually go there.”"},{"p":"Rafe looked impressed. “I do that every time I order lunch.”"},{"p":"Imani glanced at my notes. “You checked the other airport?”"},{"p":"“Before deciding, yes.”"},{"p":"She gave a small nod. Then she opened her phone and showed me a photograph of her grandmother sitting on the porch with a blanket over her knees and a ridiculous sun hat on her head."},{"p":"“Yesterday went okay,” she said. “She’s tired, but she wanted me to send you this.”"},{"p":"I smiled at the picture. “That hat is incredible.”"},{"p":"“She says it’s aerodynamic.”"},{"p":"“It has a brim the size of a helicopter rotor.”"},{"p":"“She says you should come over and test it.”"},{"p":"I looked at Imani. “Would that be okay?”"},{"p":"“Yeah,” she said. “I think it would.”"},{"p":"I didn’t ask for anything more. Across the table, Rafe was explaining why his imaginary six-engine airplane needed a swimming pool. Beckett was explaining, loudly, why it would immediately become a flying bathtub."},{"p":"For once, I had no reason to choose a side."}]},{"t":"Chapter Eleven — The Number on the Whiteboard","blocks":[{"p":"On Monday, Beckett arrived at the academy with a calculator that had enough buttons to launch a satellite."},{"p":"“Is that legal?” Rafe asked."},{"p":"“It’s a calculator.”"},{"p":"“Exactly what someone smuggling a satellite launcher would say.”"},{"p":"Beckett set it on the table with a click. “It has a memory function.”"},{"p":"“So do I,” Rafe said. “Mine remembers every embarrassing thing I’ve ever done, usually while I’m trying to sleep.”"},{"p":"Imani was already arranging her pencils by length. She looked up long enough to say, “Then yours has more storage.”"},{"p":"I laughed, and Beckett pushed a blank navigation worksheet toward me. Across the top, Mara had written a route between two fictional airports, a proposed cruising altitude, and three checkpoints. The columns underneath were familiar: course, distance, wind, groundspeed, estimated time, fuel."},{"p":"A large empty rectangle waited at the bottom."},{"p":"“What’s that for?” I asked."},{"p":"“Your explanation,” Mara said from the doorway."},{"p":"Rafe groaned. “That’s the worst rectangle.”"},{"p":"Mara put her coffee on the counter and walked to the whiteboard. She wrote 108 in blue marker."},{"p":"“Tell me what that number means.”"},{"p":"“Miles per hour?” I guessed."},{"p":"“Maybe.”"},{"p":"“Knots?” Imani asked."},{"p":"“Maybe.”"},{"p":"“The number of things Rafe will say before lunch?” Beckett offered."},{"p":"“Optimistic,” Mara said. “But you’re all proving my point. A number without a label isn’t much use.”"},{"p":"She added KTAS after the 108."},{"p":"“Now?”"},{"p":"“True airspeed,” I said."},{"p":"“Good. Does it tell you how quickly you’ll move over the ground?”"},{"p":"“Not without the wind.”"},{"p":"“And if you’re flying east with a headwind?”"},{"p":"“Groundspeed will be lower,” Imani said."},{"p":"Mara drew a little airplane and a large arrow pointing toward its nose. Her drawing looked like a paper airplane under attack by a toothbrush."},{"p":"“We’re keeping the arithmetic simple today,” she said. “Not because flight planning is simple, but because I want you to notice the assumptions. Suppose your estimated groundspeed for one leg is ninety knots. The distance is forty-five nautical miles. What’s the estimated time?”"},{"p":"Beckett had his calculator ready before I found my pencil."},{"p":"“Thirty minutes,” I said. “Forty-five divided by ninety is half an hour.”"},{"p":"“Correct. Now I erase the groundspeed.”"},{"p":"She wiped out the ninety."},{"p":"“Still thirty minutes?”"},{"p":"“No,” I said."},{"p":"“Why not?”"},{"p":"“Because we don’t know how fast we’re actually going over the ground.”"},{"p":"“You know what you hoped it would be.”"},{"p":"I glanced at my worksheet. I had already written thirty in the first time box. In pencil, thankfully."},{"p":"“I also know what I wrote down,” I admitted."},{"p":"Mara handed me the eraser. “Those aren’t always the same thing.”"},{"p":"I rubbed out the thirty. A faint gray ghost stayed on the paper. So much for a clean slate."},{"p":"For the next twenty minutes, we worked through a planning exercise using a fictional forecast and clearly marked sample performance figures. Mara made us say where each number came from. When the worksheet required aircraft fuel-burn information, she didn’t let us invent a convenient number. She showed us where a pilot would consult the particular aircraft’s approved operating information and why actual flight planning required the correct aircraft, conditions, and applicable fuel reserves."},{"p":"“Can we use the numbers from last week’s example?” Beckett asked."},{"p":"“Not unless they’re explicitly the numbers for this exercise,” she said. “Same-looking airplanes can have different equipment, loading, performance, and operating details.”"},{"p":"Rafe held up his paper. “What if I write ‘lots of fuel’?”"},{"p":"“Then you will have created a very short and very unhelpful flight plan.”"},{"p":"I was doing reasonably well until Mara announced a change in the forecast wind."},{"p":"“Your actual groundspeed is coming in below the estimate,” she said. “Not dramatically. Enough that you’ll reach the second checkpoint later than expected. What changes?”"},{"p":"“The time,” I said."},{"p":"“What else?”"},{"p":"“Our estimated arrival.”"},{"p":"“What else?”"},{"p":"I stared at the sheet. The answer sat in a column I hadn’t looked at since the beginning."},{"p":"“Fuel remaining at the later checkpoints,” I said."},{"p":"“And?”"},{"p":"“Whether the plan still gives us the margin we need.”"},{"p":"She nodded and walked on."},{"p":"I started recalculating. Then I checked it again. The second answer seemed rude. Beckett leaned over and pointed at a number."},{"p":"“You copied the distance from the wrong leg.”"},{"p":"“I did not.”"},{"p":"He tapped the heading. I looked at it. I had."},{"p":"“You absolutely did,” I said."},{"p":"“I enjoy hearing those words.”"},{"p":"“You should record them. It may never happen again.”"},{"p":"Across the table, Imani was checking her work against the sample conditions. She paused, crossed out a number, and began again without making an announcement. She crossed things out without acting as though the eraser had insulted her."},{"p":"At the break, my phone buzzed with a message from Dad. He’d sent a photograph of our refrigerator, where the family trip map was now held in place by a magnet shaped like a fish."},{"p":"Someone had drawn a crooked road across the bottom in red marker."},{"p":"Under it, Dad had written: THE FASTEST ROUTE TO THE LEFTOVER LASAGNA IS THROUGH THE KITCHEN."},{"p":"I showed it to Rafe."},{"p":"“Your dad understands navigation,” he said solemnly."},{"p":"“My dad understands leftovers.”"},{"p":"Imani leaned over to see the picture. “At least his route has a destination.”"},{"p":"I thought about the photograph she’d shown me of her grandmother in the enormous hat. I wanted to ask how things were going. Instead, I asked, “Still okay for me to visit sometime?”"},{"p":"“I told her you might come by Saturday,” she said. “She wants to know if you can carry a bag of potting soil.”"},{"p":"“I can carry a bag of potting soil.”"},{"p":"“That’s what I said. She asked whether you could do it without explaining lift.”"},{"p":"“That seems unnecessarily restrictive.”"},{"p":"Imani smiled, then returned to her worksheet."},{"p":"When class resumed, Mara put another number on the whiteboard: 12."},{"p":"“Twelve what?” she asked."},{"p":"“Minutes?” Rafe said."},{"p":"“Gallons?” Beckett said."},{"p":"“A dozen mistakes?” I offered."},{"p":"“It could be any of those. Before you make a decision using it, you need to know.”"},{"p":"She handed back our sheets. In my explanation rectangle, I’d written three sentences about verifying groundspeed and revising time estimates. She’d circled one sentence in blue: *We still have enough because the first plan said so.*"},{"p":"Beside it, she’d written: *Enough for what? Under which conditions?*"},{"p":"I stared at the question until the others began packing up."},{"p":"I used to think the worst mistake was bad arithmetic. Now I was starting to suspect the more troublesome ones were the numbers you trusted without remembering where they’d come from."},{"p":"I turned the page over and wrote Mara’s question on the back."},{"p":"Then, because I knew myself, I underlined it twice."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twelve — The Road Beyond the Field","blocks":[{"p":"On Saturday morning, Imani’s grandmother answered the door wearing the hat."},{"p":"It was yellow, with a blue ribbon, and the brim was so wide I couldn’t see the top half of the doorway behind her."},{"p":"“There he is,” she said. “The boy who still can’t fold a straight wing.”"},{"p":"“Yes, ma’am. Working on it.”"},{"p":"“Still interested in airplanes?”"},{"p":"“A little.”"},{"p":"Imani, standing behind her, made a noise that sounded like someone trying not to laugh while drinking water."},{"p":"“A little,” her grandmother repeated. “Well, come in before the sun moves and this hat starts casting a shadow over the neighbors.”"},{"p":"She had told me last summer to call her Nana June instead of Mrs. Bellweather. I was still working up to it. The house smelled of cinnamon toast and damp earth. There were plants everywhere: on the windowsill, beside the television, on a narrow shelf above the kitchen sink. Some were thriving. Others looked as if Nana June had already given them a stern warning."},{"p":"“These three are being dramatic,” Nana June told me, pointing to a row of drooping leaves. “They were moved yesterday and have taken it personally.”"},{"p":"Imani took my jacket and hung it on a hook. “The soil’s on the porch.”"},{"p":"“I was promised physical labor,” I said."},{"p":"“You were promised nothing,” she replied. “You volunteered.”"},{"p":"The bag of potting soil was heavier than it looked. I lifted it carefully and carried it around the side of the house to a raised garden bed. Nana June followed at a slower pace, one hand resting on the railing where the path sloped. She stopped halfway to catch her breath. Imani didn’t rush her or pretend not to notice. She simply waited beside her, talking about whether the tomatoes had enough sun."},{"p":"I balanced the bag against my hip, caught between offering help and staying out of the way."},{"p":"Nana June looked at me. “You can put that down, young man. The garden isn’t leaving.”"},{"p":"I set it beside the bed."},{"p":"“I appreciate a helper who can follow instructions,” she said. “You’d be surprised how rare that is.”"},{"p":"“He has trouble with the not-talking part,” Imani said."},{"p":"“I’m working on it.”"},{"p":"“Progress is visible,” Nana June said."},{"p":"We spent an hour planting herbs. My job was to hold the seedling tray. It felt easy until I tipped it and nearly launched six basil plants onto the patio."},{"p":"“Easy,” Nana June said, steadying the tray. “They’re not passengers on a roller coaster.”"},{"p":"“Sorry.”"},{"p":"“No harm done. Now look at the roots before you move the next one.”"},{"p":"She showed me how to loosen the seedling from its container without tearing it apart. Her fingers were slower than Imani’s but more certain. When I started to press the soil down too hard, she stopped me."},{"p":"“You don’t have to win an argument with it.”"},{"p":"“I wasn’t trying to.”"},{"p":"“Your thumbs were.”"},{"p":"Imani laughed so hard she had to sit on the step."},{"p":"By noon, the garden bed held basil, parsley, and a row of small marigolds Nana June said were there because she liked them, not because every plant needed a practical excuse."},{"p":"We ate sandwiches on the porch. Imani brought out three glasses of lemonade, and Nana June told us about getting lost on a family road trip when she was younger. Her father had refused to stop and ask directions because he was convinced the next turn would fix everything."},{"p":"“Did it?” I asked."},{"p":"“Eventually,” she said. “The next turn was the entrance to a farm. We met a very judgmental goat.”"},{"p":"“Did you ask the goat for directions?”"},{"p":"“We should have. It knew where it lived.”"},{"p":"I looked at Imani. “Rafe would love that story.”"},{"p":"“Rafe would spend the rest of the afternoon designing a goat-shaped GPS.”"},{"p":"Nana June studied us over her glass. “You two talk about being lost quite a lot.”"},{"p":"“It’s what we’re learning at the academy,” I said. “Well, mostly how not to be lost. And what to do if the plan stops matching what you see.”"},{"p":"“That sounds useful beyond airplanes.”"},{"p":"I waited for her to explain what she meant. She didn’t. Instead, she asked whether I wanted another sandwich."},{"p":"I did."},{"p":"Later, Imani and I carried the empty tray into the kitchen. She rinsed it while I dried the counter with a towel that had tiny embroidered strawberries on it."},{"p":"“Thanks for coming,” she said."},{"p":"“Thanks for inviting me. Your grandmother’s still undefeated.”"},{"p":"“She has good days and bad days. Today was a good one.”"},{"p":"I nodded. I wanted to say I hoped every day would be good from now on. I knew that wasn’t something either of us could promise."},{"p":"“I’m glad I got to see her,” I said."},{"p":"Imani looked at me for a moment. “Me too.”"},{"p":"On Monday, the sky above Hampton Roads Executive was pale blue, with scattered clouds moving east. Mara met us by the briefing table with a chart, a ruler, and a small stack of index cards."},{"p":"“Today we’re talking about what happens when the next checkpoint doesn’t show up when you expect it,” she said."},{"p":"“We panic?” Rafe suggested."},{"p":"“You, personally, may feel tempted. That’s not the procedure.”"},{"p":"She laid out a route for a supervised practice flight. It was a different one from the last lesson, with checkpoints selected so that we could identify them from the airplane under suitable conditions. She reviewed the current weather and the flight’s limits with me, then explained how the exercise would work. She would remain the instructor in command, and if she introduced a navigation problem, she could take the controls while I worked it out."},{"p":"“First priority?” she asked."},{"p":"“Fly the airplane,” I said."},{"p":"“Then?”"},{"p":"“Keep track of where we are. Use the information we have. Ask for help early if we need it.”"},{"p":"“Good. A simulated problem is still an exercise. We’re not going to manufacture a real emergency to make it interesting.”"},{"p":"Rafe looked disappointed. “There goes my screenplay.”"},{"p":"I was in the right seat of the classroom table, not the airplane, when she turned over the first index card. It said: *Expected checkpoint not visible. Groundspeed lower than planned.*"},{"p":"“What do you do?”"},{"p":"“Tell you,” I said. “Confirm our last known position and time. Check what landmarks we can positively identify. Reassess the estimate instead of chasing the old one.”"},{"p":"“And if you’re uncertain?”"},{"p":"“Say so.”"},{"p":"She turned over the next card: *Fuel margin now less comfortable than planned.*"},{"p":"I hesitated. I could hear the number twelve from last week’s whiteboard in my head, still waiting for its units."},{"p":"“We don’t just keep going because we already started,” I said. “We check the actual situation, consider suitable alternatives, and make a decision with enough margin.”"},{"p":"“Good. What would you need to verify?”"},{"p":"I started listing things: current position, conditions, usable airports, aircraft information, fuel estimates, and whether the revised plan was still appropriate. Mara made me slow down and distinguish what we knew from what we still needed to check."},{"p":"At the end, she slid the cards into a neat stack."},{"p":"“We’ll take this into the airplane on the next suitable day,” she said. “Today we plan.”"},{"p":"I felt a brief sting of disappointment. I’d worn my lucky socks, the ones with little blue airplanes on them, and nobody had even seen them."},{"p":"“Could we have flown?” I asked."},{"p":"“Possibly. But a lesson doesn’t have to happen in the air to move you forward.”"},{"p":"Outside the window, a maintenance truck crossed the apron. Beyond it, the airport fence ran beside a field of summer grass. A narrow road curved past the far edge, disappearing behind a line of trees."},{"p":"From here, I couldn’t see where it went."},{"p":"I remembered Nana June’s garden, and the way she’d made me stop pressing the soil so hard. I remembered how her father had driven past the turn because he couldn’t bear to admit he needed directions. I remembered Imani waiting on the sloped path without hurrying her grandmother."},{"p":"“You okay?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“Yeah,” I said. “I was thinking about something.”"},{"p":"“Want to share?”"},{"p":"I looked at the road beyond the field."},{"p":"“Sometimes when you can’t see what’s next,” I said, “you can still do something useful where you are.”"},{"p":"Mara considered that. “What are you going to do that’s useful?”"},{"p":"I pulled the chart closer and reached for my pencil. “Check the route again.”"},{"p":"This time, I started with the part we knew."}]},{"t":"Chapter Thirteen — The Wind Had Other Plans","blocks":[{"p":"The first sign that Tuesday wasn’t going to follow my plan was the toaster."},{"p":"It launched my breakfast halfway across the kitchen, where it landed butter-side down on Dad’s library book."},{"p":"Dad looked at the toast. Then at the book. Then at me."},{"p":"“I think,” he said, “the universe is suggesting you slow down.”"},{"p":"“The universe could have sent an email.”"},{"p":"Mom handed me a paper towel. “The universe knows you don’t read those.”"},{"p":"I cleaned the cover carefully. Naturally, it was a book about maps. Mara and I had a supervised navigation flight planned, and I had already checked the weather twice before breakfast. The sky looked blue through the kitchen window. The forecast had looked promising the night before. My chart was packed, my pencil was sharp, and my socks had tiny blue airplanes on them."},{"p":"I was ready."},{"p":"Or at least I had packed everything that could make me feel ready."},{"p":"At the airport, Beckett was sitting at the briefing table with a bag of pretzels and an expression of personal betrayal."},{"p":"“My bicycle tire went flat,” he said. “I walked the last mile. If this is what cross-country travel is like, I prefer staying home.”"},{"p":"“You could have called,” I said."},{"p":"“I did. You were busy sending me pictures of your socks.”"},{"p":"“They’re lucky.”"},{"p":"“They’re socks.”"},{"p":"Rafe leaned over the table. “As the only person here who appreciates advanced technology, I support the socks.”"},{"p":"Imani was already studying the weather display. “You might want to look at this before you start celebrating.”"},{"p":"I set my bag down. The latest airport weather report showed wind that had shifted and strengthened since early morning. The sustained wind alone didn’t settle anything, but the reported gusts got my attention. The forecast discussion anticipated more changes through the morning. The clouds on the updated display looked different from last night’s."},{"p":"“That’s not what it said at six,” I muttered."},{"p":"“It isn’t six anymore,” Imani said."},{"p":"She didn’t sound smug. That made it harder to be annoyed with her."},{"p":"Mara joined us carrying the aircraft paperwork. “Tell me what changed.”"},{"p":"I gave her the weather report, then pointed to the planned route. “The wind is stronger than I used in the calculation. Our groundspeed estimate could change. And the gusts might affect whether today’s lesson is suitable.”"},{"p":"“What else?”"},{"p":"“The observation is for here. We need to look at the conditions along the route, not just the departure airport.”"},{"p":"She nodded. “And what does the weather look like at our planned time, not the time you hoped we’d leave?”"},{"p":"I felt my ears warm. I’d planned for a departure time that now seemed optimistic. I pulled out the chart and checked the route against the new information. Mara asked me to walk her through my assumptions. I had used a wind estimate from the previous evening to calculate a heading and time between checkpoints. It had been a reasonable starting point. It wasn’t a permanent agreement with the atmosphere."},{"p":"We reviewed the updated information, the aircraft’s operating limits, and Mara’s training limits. The gusts weren’t the only factor; the lesson involved navigation tasks that would divide my attention. She made the final decision as the instructor in command."},{"p":"“Not this morning,” she said."},{"p":"I stared at the blue patch of sky beyond the window."},{"p":"“I know,” Mara said. “It’s frustrating.”"},{"p":"“It’s just that the weather doesn’t look terrible.”"},{"p":"“It doesn’t have to look terrible for a particular lesson to be the wrong choice.”"},{"p":"I wanted to argue that I could handle it. The problem was that I couldn’t actually prove that by arguing."},{"p":"“Could we change the lesson?” I asked."},{"p":"“We can work on the plan. And if conditions improve later, we’ll reassess rather than promise ourselves a flight.”"},{"p":"Beckett slid the pretzels toward me like an offering at a funeral."},{"p":"“For your loss,” he said."},{"p":"“Thank you. Please respect my privacy during this difficult time.”"},{"p":"We spread the chart across the table. Mara had me compare my original estimates with revised ones using a wind triangle example, then check what the changes meant for time and fuel planning. She didn’t let me treat the result as a magic number. We still needed an actual briefing, suitable conditions, and a decision appropriate for the airplane and the flight."},{"p":"Rafe drew an angry cloud on a sticky note and attached it to my flight bag."},{"p":"“Suspect remains at large,” he said."},{"p":"Imani looked at my worksheet. “You crossed out the original groundspeed.”"},{"p":"“Because it’s wrong now.”"},{"p":"“It wasn’t necessarily wrong when you calculated it.”"},{"p":"I paused. That was the part I kept missing. A plan could be carefully made and still need changing. Updating it didn’t erase the work that came before."},{"p":"“I’ll write the new estimate beside it,” I said."},{"p":"“And the time you checked it,” she added."},{"p":"By eleven, the gusts were still arriving in uneven bursts. A windsock near the runway snapped outward, drooped, and snapped again. Mara didn’t reopen the flight decision. Instead, she asked us to design a short briefing for the younger students who would visit that afternoon."},{"p":"Rafe titled our presentation THE WEATHER HAS DECLINED YOUR INVITATION."},{"p":"Mara made him remove the word *declined* because, she said, weather wasn’t making decisions for us."},{"p":"“Fine,” Rafe said. “THE WEATHER HAS LEFT US ON READ.”"},{"p":"She let that one stay on the practice sheet, provided he explained the actual lesson afterward."},{"p":"When I got home, Dad had managed to clean the butter from his book. He held it up when I walked in."},{"p":"“Good news. The history of cartography has survived.”"},{"p":"“The flight didn’t happen.”"},{"p":"He put the book down. “Sorry, Link.”"},{"p":"I sat across from him and told him about the wind. He listened while I explained the numbers, then the revised plan, then the part where I wished Mara had made a different decision."},{"p":"“Sounds like you did a lot of work,” he said."},{"p":"“Without going anywhere.”"},{"p":"He tapped the book cover. “Most maps are made before anybody starts moving. Doesn’t mean the work isn’t real.”"},{"p":"I looked at the faint buttery mark near the spine."},{"p":"“Are you going to use that for every conversation now?”"},{"p":"“Only until you replace my breakfast book.”"},{"p":"That evening, I unfolded the chart again. I wrote the revised calculations neatly, dated them, and tucked the older worksheet behind the new one. I didn’t throw it away."},{"p":"The route was still there. The airplane was still there. I still wanted to fly."},{"p":"Tomorrow would have its own weather, and I would have to meet it when it arrived."}]},{"t":"Chapter Fourteen — The Space Between Checkpoints","blocks":[{"p":"On Thursday morning, Mara didn’t ask me whether I was excited."},{"p":"She asked me where my pencil was."},{"p":"“Right here,” I said, holding it up."},{"p":"“Backup?”"},{"p":"I reached into the side pocket of my bag. Empty."},{"p":"Rafe, who had come to see us off, produced a pencil from behind my ear."},{"p":"“You were carrying it all along,” he said in a terrible magician voice."},{"p":"“That was not there.”"},{"p":"“Aviation is full of mysteries.”"},{"p":"Mara took the pencil, inspected the eraser, and gave it back. “A second pencil won’t make you a better pilot, Lincoln. It helps when the first one rolls under a seat.”"},{"p":"I put it in my kneeboard. This time, I had a current briefing, a revised route, and a weather picture that Mara judged suitable for our planned dual-instruction lesson. We’d discussed alternates, time and fuel expectations, and the point at which we’d stop the exercise if things stopped making sense. Mara was the instructor and pilot in command; she could take the controls whenever needed."},{"p":"I wanted a perfect cross-country lesson. Mara wanted me to keep track of the airplane, the map, and what I actually knew."},{"p":"After the preflight inspection, we climbed into the Cessna. The cabin smelled faintly of warm vinyl and paper. I adjusted my seat and checked the belt. Mara went through the departure plan with me, including who would handle the radios and how we’d divide the workload. We used the checklist. Mara supervised the taxi and departure procedures."},{"p":"When we were airborne and clear of the busiest part of the departure, the familiar airport shrank into the landscape. Roads crossed the fields like pencil lines. Water flashed between stands of trees. From the ground, I knew the route to the airport by every turn and traffic light. From up here, the same place looked like somebody had rearranged the furniture."},{"p":"“First checkpoint?” Mara asked."},{"p":"I named a highway interchange that stood out on the chart and should be easy to identify from our planned track. I checked the time, looked outside, and found two roads that might have been the ones I wanted."},{"p":"“I see an interchange,” I said."},{"p":"“Which one?”"},{"p":"I opened my mouth. Then closed it."},{"p":"One of the roads curved toward a small pond. On the chart, the interchange I wanted had a rail line farther east. I couldn’t see the rail line yet."},{"p":"“I’m not ready to call it,” I said."},{"p":"“Good. Keep flying. What can you use to verify it?”"},{"p":"I checked the chart again and searched for the rail line and the nearby bend in the river. Mara monitored the aircraft and prompted me to keep my scan moving. A moment later, the pieces matched. The interchange was farther ahead than the first one I’d seen."},{"p":"“There,” I said. “That one. The rail line is beyond it, and the river bend is off to the side where I expected.”"},{"p":"“Time?”"},{"p":"I marked it."},{"p":"The little pencil dot wasn’t impressive. It was smaller than a freckle. But I had earned it by waiting until I was sure."},{"p":"Between checkpoints, the airplane hummed steadily. I compared our progress with the estimate and noticed we weren’t arriving exactly when my worksheet predicted. I checked the time again, then told Mara what I was seeing."},{"p":"“What might explain it?” she asked."},{"p":"“Our actual groundspeed could differ from the estimate. The wind might not match what we planned. Or I might have marked a time incorrectly.”"},{"p":"“And what shouldn’t you do?”"},{"p":"“Pretend the next checkpoint is closer because I want it to be.”"},{"p":"She smiled. “That’s one possibility.”"},{"p":"The next landmark was a reservoir shaped, according to Rafe, like a chicken attempting to escape a frying pan. I had thought the description ridiculous until I saw the water from above. Then I had to admit the chicken was making a strong effort."},{"p":"I located the reservoir, confirmed the road crossing at its northern edge, and marked our progress. I was starting to feel the rhythm of it: look outside, maintain control, check the chart, confirm, update. It wasn’t as smooth as I’d imagined. My pencil occasionally caught on the paper. I had to remind myself not to stare down at the kneeboard. Mara took the controls briefly while I reorganized the chart."},{"p":"“Your airplane,” she said when I was ready."},{"p":"“My airplane.”"},{"p":"The words felt good, but they didn’t mean I was alone. Mara was beside me, watching and teaching, and that mattered."},{"p":"A few minutes later, she pointed toward a line of trees."},{"p":"“Let’s say your next planned checkpoint isn’t visible. For the exercise, tell me how you’d handle it.”"},{"p":"I looked at the chart. The road we expected was partly hidden by the trees, and another road ran almost parallel to it. I could feel myself wanting to pick one."},{"p":"“I’d tell you I’m uncertain,” I said. “Use our last confirmed position, heading, and elapsed time as a starting point. Then find another feature that can confirm where we are.”"},{"p":"“Go on.”"},{"p":"“If we can’t establish our position confidently, we don’t just keep wandering after a guess. We’d work together, use the available navigation equipment, and get assistance if needed.”"},{"p":"“All right. Show me what you can actually identify.”"},{"p":"I found a long industrial roof near a road junction. I compared it with the chart and with the position displayed on the GPS. The picture began to fit, but not perfectly. I said that out loud, too."},{"p":"“Good,” Mara said. “An uncertain position is information. It’s not a character flaw.”"},{"p":"We continued the planned exercise only after she was satisfied that we had our position and enough margin to proceed. At the next point, she introduced a simulated change: imagine the destination’s weather was becoming unsuitable. We discussed the options without pretending a fictional problem was an actual emergency. She had me identify a suitable alternate from information we’d reviewed on the ground and explain what else would need to be checked before any real diversion."},{"p":"I chose one, then caught myself."},{"p":"“I need to confirm the current conditions and airport details,” I said. “And we have to account for fuel and where we actually are.”"},{"p":"“Exactly. An airport name on a chart isn’t the whole answer.”"},{"p":"We turned toward home as planned. The return leg was quieter. I was tired in a way that had nothing to do with running or carrying heavy things. My brain felt like it had spent the morning holding open twelve drawers at once."},{"p":"Mara handled more of the radio work while I concentrated on flying and locating our next reference. The airport eventually came back into view, familiar and oddly new. We completed the arrival and landing with Mara supervising, then taxied in and shut down according to the checklist."},{"p":"Only after the engine stopped did I notice how hard I’d been gripping my pencil."},{"p":"“How’d it feel?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“Like I knew where I was for about eighty percent of it.”"},{"p":"“That’s a very specific estimate.”"},{"p":"“I made it up.”"},{"p":"“Then perhaps we won’t put it in the logbook.”"},{"p":"Outside, Beckett and Rafe were waiting by the classroom door. Imani stood with them, her braids moving in the breeze."},{"p":"“Did you find the chicken reservoir?” Rafe called."},{"p":"“Yes. It was fleeing north.”"},{"p":"Beckett held up a folded sheet of paper. “We made you a certificate.”"},{"p":"Across the top, in large crooked letters, it said: *HONORARY IDENTIFIER OF THINGS THAT ARE ACTUALLY THERE.* Underneath was a drawing of an airplane with six wings and a suspiciously human face."},{"p":"“That’s awful,” I said."},{"p":"“You’re welcome,” Beckett replied."},{"p":"Imani waited until the others started arguing about whether the drawing was aerodynamically possible."},{"p":"“How was it?” she asked."},{"p":"“Good. Harder than I expected. I got some things right, and a few things took longer than they should have.”"},{"p":"“Did you have to change the plan?”"},{"p":"“We updated the estimates, and Mara gave me a simulated problem. I kept wanting to answer before I’d checked everything.”"},{"p":"“Sounds familiar.”"},{"p":"“Yeah. Apparently my brain thinks uncertainty is a personal insult.”"},{"p":"She laughed. “It’ll get over it.”"},{"p":"I hoped she was right."},{"p":"At home, I laid the chart on the dining table. Dad came over and examined the pencil marks. He didn’t ask how fast we’d gone or whether I’d done anything exciting. He asked what one of the small circles meant."},{"p":"“That’s where I was sure of our position,” I told him."},{"p":"He pointed to the space between two circles. “And here?”"},{"p":"I looked at the unmarked stretch of paper. Earlier in the week, I might have called it empty. Now I remembered the roads that looked alike, the water that resembled a chicken, and the moment I had told Mara I wasn’t ready to identify a checkpoint."},{"p":"“That’s where I had to keep checking,” I said."},{"p":"Dad nodded, as though that explained more than I’d expected."},{"p":"I kept the certificate from Beckett and Rafe. I folded it carefully and slipped it behind my chart, next to the older weather worksheet."},{"p":"Neither one was something I’d ever show a real examiner."},{"p":"But both reminded me of something I wanted to remember the next time the world didn’t match my pencil line."}]},{"t":"Chapter Fifteen — Four Pencils, One Map","blocks":[{"p":"Beckett brought a ruler to the team project. That should have warned me."},{"p":"The second warning was that he brought two."},{"p":"“One for measuring,” he explained, laying them beside his notebook, “and one for when somebody borrows the first one and forgets what a straight line is.”"},{"p":"Rafe, who was eating a granola bar with the concentration of a professional food critic, held out his hand."},{"p":"“Can I borrow the second one?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Excellent system.”"},{"p":"It was Thursday afternoon at the academy. Rain clicked against the classroom windows, turning the view of the ramp into a blurry arrangement of parked airplanes and orange cones. Mara had given us an assignment that sounded simple until she handed over the packet."},{"p":"Four students. One fictional daytime cross-country route. A destination, a fuel stop, two possible alternates, and several ways for the plan to unravel. We had to build a planning presentation for the younger academy group, explaining not just the route we chose but how we would recognize when it was time to change it."},{"p":"“This is a classroom scenario,” Mara reminded us. “You’re not dispatching a flight. Use the fictional information in the packet. In real flying, you’d use current charts, weather, aircraft data, and proper briefings with your instructor.”"},{"p":"I nodded. I had already decided I would handle navigation."},{"p":"Beckett had apparently decided the same thing."},{"p":"“I’ll lay out the route and distances,” he said, sliding the chart toward himself."},{"p":"“I can do that,” I said."},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“Then why are you doing it?”"},{"p":"“Because I’m holding the chart.”"},{"p":"Rafe lifted the granola bar. “As the person currently holding the snacks, I declare myself catering director.”"},{"p":"Imani turned the packet over. “We should decide what the presentation needs before dividing it up.”"},{"p":"“We know what it needs,” I said. “Route, checkpoints, time, fuel, alternates.”"},{"p":"“And what might make us change our minds,” she added."},{"p":"“Right.”"},{"p":"I reached for the chart. Beckett kept one finger on it."},{"p":"“I can mark the checkpoints,” I said."},{"p":"“You marked a chicken-shaped reservoir last week,” Rafe said. “I think that qualifies you.”"},{"p":"“Thank you.”"},{"p":"“It was a compliment with conditions.”"},{"p":"Beckett finally let go. I spread the chart flat, lined up the first leg, and drew a pencil mark. For ten minutes we worked almost peacefully. I identified recognizable features along the route. Beckett checked the distances. Rafe made a tidy table for planned times. Imani reviewed the weather changes Mara had placed in the packet."},{"p":"Then Imani said, “We should choose the southern alternate.”"},{"p":"I looked up. “Why?”"},{"p":"“The northern one has the shorter distance, but the later weather update makes the southern option more useful.”"},{"p":"“The northern airport is closer to the route.”"},{"p":"“I know. I said the southern one is more useful.”"},{"p":"I studied the page. The simulated weather at the northern airport had been acceptable in the morning. A later update showed a lowering ceiling and visibility trending down. The southern airport’s fictional conditions remained better, though reaching it would require a different turn and more distance."},{"p":"“We can list both,” I said. “No reason to throw away the closer one.”"},{"p":"“We can list both as possibilities,” Imani said. “But if we’re explaining a decision at the later time, we shouldn’t pretend they are equally good.”"},{"p":"Beckett tapped his ruler on the chart. “And you can’t compare the extra distance without checking the fuel figures.”"},{"p":"“I know that.”"},{"p":"“Then let me finish the distance table before you circle anything.”"},{"p":"“You don’t need to guard the chart like it’s a museum painting.”"},{"p":"He sat back. “And you don’t need to take over every time you have a pencil.”"},{"p":"Rafe stopped chewing."},{"p":"I hated how familiar that sentence sounded. We’d already had a version of this argument over a weight-and-balance worksheet. I had apologized then. I thought that meant we were finished having it."},{"p":"“I’m trying to keep us moving,” I said."},{"p":"“You’re trying to keep us moving your way,” Beckett replied."},{"p":"Mara was helping another table. I could have called her over. Instead I picked up my pencil and drew a circle around the northern alternate."},{"p":"“There. We have a starting point.”"},{"p":"Imani stared at the circle."},{"p":"“Lincoln,” she said quietly, “I was still talking.”"},{"p":"I opened my mouth. I had several excellent defenses prepared. We had limited time. The circle was erasable. We needed something on paper. Every defense sounded reasonable. None explained why I hadn’t let her finish."},{"p":"“Sorry,” I said."},{"p":"She nodded once, but she didn’t pick up the conversation."},{"p":"We worked in silence for a while. Not the productive kind. We shuffled papers to avoid looking at each other."},{"p":"When Mara stopped by, she studied our chart and the separate pages scattered around it."},{"p":"“How’s the team doing?”"},{"p":"“We have a route,” I said."},{"p":"“We have three versions of a route,” Beckett corrected."},{"p":"“And an alternate that may not be suitable at the time of the decision,” Imani said."},{"p":"Rafe held up his table. “I have beautiful handwriting and no idea which distances belong in it.”"},{"p":"Mara pulled out a chair, but she didn’t sit."},{"p":"“What did you agree the decision was?”"},{"p":"We all started answering at once."},{"p":"She waited until we stopped."},{"p":"“That,” she said, “might be worth solving before the arithmetic.”"},{"p":"She walked away to check on the other group."},{"p":"I stared at my circle. It looked childish now, like a sticker I’d put on something to claim it."},{"p":"“Okay,” I said. “We need one decision point. The later weather update. We decide whether to continue the fictional route or divert, and explain why.”"},{"p":"“Yes,” Imani said."},{"p":"“We check both alternates against the information we have at that time,” Beckett added."},{"p":"“And the distance and fuel numbers have to match the route we actually choose,” Rafe said."},{"p":"I looked at him."},{"p":"“What?” he asked. “Sometimes I listen while chewing.”"},{"p":"I erased the circle. The eraser left a pale smudge. The airport symbol survived."},{"p":"“Imani, would you walk us through the weather update? All the way through. I won’t interrupt.”"},{"p":"She looked at me long enough that I felt the full weight of my earlier performance."},{"p":"Then she turned the packet toward the middle of the table."},{"p":"“All right. At the first time, both airports look possible in this exercise. At the later time, we need to reassess. The northern report is getting worse. We shouldn’t choose it just because it’s closer.”"},{"p":"Beckett started a new distance column. Rafe copied the updated decision time onto his sheet. I drew nothing until we’d agreed what the line should represent."},{"p":"It took longer. This time, all four pages agreed."},{"p":"Near the end of class, Mara asked each group to explain one disagreement and how they’d resolved it."},{"p":"Rafe raised his hand before any of us could stop him."},{"p":"“We discovered that our main navigation hazard was Lincoln’s pencil.”"},{"p":"Several students laughed. My ears got hot."},{"p":"“He’s not wrong,” I said."},{"p":"Mara looked at me, waiting."},{"p":"“I marked an answer before we’d agreed what question we were answering. It made the rest of the work harder. We erased it and started with the decision point.”"},{"p":"Beckett slid his finished table across the desk. “The second version was better.”"},{"p":"“Because of the ruler?” Rafe asked."},{"p":"“Because we checked each other.”"},{"p":"Imani gave the smallest smile."},{"p":"On the way out, I held the classroom door while she adjusted the strap of her bag."},{"p":"“I really am sorry I interrupted you,” I said."},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“I keep thinking I’m better at that now.”"},{"p":"“Sometimes you are.”"},{"p":"It wasn’t a perfect answer. It was a fair one."},{"p":"Outside, the rain had stopped. Water dripped from the edge of the hangar roof into a shallow puddle. Beckett stepped around it; Rafe jumped directly into the middle and soaked one shoe."},{"p":"“I regret nothing,” he announced."},{"p":"He regretted it all the way to the parking lot."}]},{"t":"Chapter Sixteen — The Wrong Kind of Right","blocks":[{"p":"By Saturday morning, our presentation had acquired a title, a crooked airplane drawing, and an argument about whether the word *alternate* needed a bigger font."},{"p":"“It’s the most important word,” Beckett insisted."},{"p":"“The most important word is *snacks*,” Rafe said."},{"p":"“There are no snacks on the poster.”"},{"p":"“An oversight I’m willing to correct.”"},{"p":"We were setting up for the younger academy students in the classroom beside the hangar. Dad had dropped me off early because he was covering a Saturday shift at the library. He’d handed me a paper bag on the way out."},{"p":"“Lunch,” he’d said. “And a reminder that other people are allowed to have ideas.”"},{"p":"“Mom told you about the project?”"},{"p":"“You told me about the project. For fifteen minutes. While explaining why everyone else was wrong.”"},{"p":"“I did not say everyone was wrong.”"},{"p":"“You used the phrase ‘numerically inconvenient.’”"},{"p":"I couldn’t argue with that. Unfortunately, I had."},{"p":"Now the four of us stood in front of our display while a handful of younger students filed into the room. The youngest was a boy in a red sweatshirt who immediately pointed at Rafe’s airplane drawing."},{"p":"“Why does it have a smile?”"},{"p":"“Because it hasn’t seen our fuel worksheet,” Rafe said."},{"p":"Mara gave him a look."},{"p":"“Because I’m a terrible artist,” he amended."},{"p":"Our assignment was to explain a fictional flight-planning problem in a way a younger student could understand. Our job wasn’t to teach them to make flight decisions. It was to show them how to ask questions, check information, and know when to involve an instructor."},{"p":"Imani began with the weather cards. She showed two reports for the same fictional airport, separated by time."},{"p":"“What’s different?” she asked."},{"p":"The boy in red pointed to a number."},{"p":"“That one got smaller.”"},{"p":"“Good catch. It’s the reported visibility in this exercise. What else changed?”"},{"p":"A girl with a purple hair clip spotted the ceiling information. Imani praised her for looking at the whole report rather than one number."},{"p":"Beckett moved to the distance table. He used string laid across the chart to compare the two alternates without pretending either choice was automatically correct. Rafe explained that a plan is based on estimates and has to be updated when information changes."},{"p":"Then it was my turn."},{"p":"I held up our final decision sheet. “So we picked the southern alternate for this fictional situation, because the later weather information made it a more suitable option— assuming the other checks supported it.”"},{"p":"The girl with the purple hair clip raised her hand."},{"p":"“What if the southern airport has no fuel?”"},{"p":"I glanced at the packet. It didn’t say."},{"p":"“Then we’d need to know whether fuel services were actually required for the plan,” I said, “and whether that airport would still be suitable. We’d check the airport information rather than guess.”"},{"p":"“What if your airplane is already low on gas?”"},{"p":"“That changes the situation a lot,” I said. “You plan for alternatives before fuel becomes a problem.”"},{"p":"Beckett held up the worksheet. “Our exercise includes planned fuel and a reserve, but those figures depend on the assumptions we used.”"},{"p":"“And in a real airplane,” I added, “Mara would review the real flight plan with us and decide whether the flight was safe to make.”"},{"p":"The boy in red frowned at the chart."},{"p":"“Why didn’t you just go to the first airport?”"},{"p":"“Because it was closer?” I asked."},{"p":"He nodded."},{"p":"An entire speech about weather and distance lined up in my head. It even came with diagrams."},{"p":"Instead I asked, “Have you ever been on a road trip when the nearest gas station was closed?”"},{"p":"He nodded again, harder."},{"p":"“My dad said a bad word.”"},{"p":"“Then you already understand part of it. The closest place isn’t always the place that can help you.”"},{"p":"He considered this."},{"p":"“My dad said two bad words.”"},{"p":"Rafe made a strangled noise behind the poster."},{"p":"The presentation went well until the final question. A student near the back asked what we’d do if two team members disagreed about which airport to choose."},{"p":"I looked at Beckett. Beckett looked at Imani. Rafe studied the ceiling as though it contained an escape hatch."},{"p":"“We actually did disagree,” I said."},{"p":"The room got quiet."},{"p":"“I thought the closest alternate should be our starting answer. Imani wanted us to look harder at the updated weather. Beckett needed us to settle the route before he could check the numbers. Rafe was trying to make one table out of three different plans.”"},{"p":"“Four,” Rafe said. “At one point there were four.”"},{"p":"“Four,” I admitted. “I thought if I made a decision quickly, we’d get the project done faster. But I hadn’t listened to everybody’s information.”"},{"p":"The girl with the purple clip raised her hand again."},{"p":"“So who was the boss?”"},{"p":"“Mara,” all four of us said together."},{"p":"Mara, standing by the doorway, laughed."},{"p":"“For the classroom, yes,” she said. “But a good team also needs a way to hear concerns before a decision is made. In an actual flight, the pilot in command has defined responsibilities. That doesn’t make useful information from other people less important.”"},{"p":"When the visitors left, Mara asked us to stay a few minutes."},{"p":"I expected a review of the poster. Instead she set a folded planning sheet on the table."},{"p":"“You’ve all done well with the exercise,” she said. “Next week we’ll look at a more complete supervised cross-country lesson. It will depend on aircraft availability, weather, your preparation, and my judgment. Nothing is guaranteed.”"},{"p":"My stomach performed a small, ridiculous jump."},{"p":"“A longer route?” I asked."},{"p":"“A more complete one. You’ll prepare the navigation plan and walk me through your decisions. We’ll review everything together before deciding whether to fly.”"},{"p":"Beckett leaned forward. “Can we help with the ground preparation?”"},{"p":"“You can work through practice scenarios together,” Mara said. “Lincoln’s actual flight planning and decisions will be reviewed with me. And each of you will have your own training milestones.”"},{"p":"I glanced at Imani. She was looking at the folded sheet, not at me."},{"p":"“You should take the first look,” I told her."},{"p":"She raised an eyebrow. “Why?”"},{"p":"“Because if I grab it, Beckett will file a formal complaint against my pencil.”"},{"p":"“Already drafted,” Beckett said."},{"p":"Imani unfolded the sheet and placed it in the center of the table where all four of us could see it."},{"p":"That evening, Mom found me at the dining table among pencils, notes, and an untouched sandwich. I was studying the route Mara had given me, tracing one possible checkpoint and then another."},{"p":"“Big week coming?” she asked."},{"p":"“Maybe. Depends on a lot of things.”"},{"p":"“You sound disappointed about something that hasn’t happened yet.”"},{"p":"“I’m trying not to get ahead of myself.”"},{"p":"She leaned over my shoulder. “How’s the group?”"},{"p":"I thought of Imani letting me finish my explanation, Beckett checking my numbers without making a speech, and Rafe telling an eight-year-old that an airplane smiled because it hadn’t seen the fuel worksheet."},{"p":"“Better,” I said. “We had to start over once.”"},{"p":"“Only once?”"},{"p":"“Don’t make it sound easy.”"},{"p":"She kissed the top of my head, which I pretended to find deeply embarrassing, and went back to the kitchen."},{"p":"My phone buzzed. A message from Imani appeared."},{"p":"*Beckett says the southern route table is fixed. Also Nana June wants to know if your tomato plant is still alive.*"},{"p":"I looked toward the windowsill. The little plant she’d sent home with me had one drooping leaf and a new green shoot."},{"p":"*Alive,* I typed. *Possibly offended by my watering technique.*"},{"p":"Three dots appeared, disappeared, then returned."},{"p":"*She says that’s how she knows it’s yours.*"},{"p":"I laughed. I wanted to ask about Nana June. I left the question alone."},{"p":"A minute later another message arrived."},{"p":"*Good luck with the planning. And don’t forget to let the other people talk.*"},{"p":"I wrote back: *I’m putting that on my kneeboard.*"},{"p":"I turned back to the chart. Beckett had circled a checkpoint I’d overlooked. I added it to my notes."},{"p":"For the first time all week, the four of us seemed to be heading in the same direction."}]},{"t":"Chapter Seventeen — The Quietest Voice","blocks":[{"p":"Nobody touched the map. That was how I knew our team had improved."},{"p":"Four of us stood around the table at Hype Teen Flight Academy, looking at the route Mara had assigned for our final group planning challenge. It ran from Hampton Roads Executive toward a fictional inland destination, with two possible alternates and weather reports that refused to cooperate."},{"p":"Normally I would have picked up a pencil before everybody sat down. This time I kept my hands in my pockets."},{"p":"Rafe noticed immediately."},{"p":"“Are you injured?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Have your hands been confiscated?”"},{"p":"“I’m practicing restraint.”"},{"p":"“Is that contagious?” Beckett asked. “Because I have a list of people.”"},{"p":"Imani set her notebook beside the chart. “Before anyone draws anything, can we agree on what the assignment is?”"},{"p":"I could practically hear Mara applauding from somewhere behind a closed office door."},{"p":"“Build a defensible plan,” I said. “Not the fastest plan. Not the prettiest plan.”"},{"p":"“And identify what we don’t know,” Imani added."},{"p":"Beckett uncapped his pen. “Aircraft performance figures are provided for the exercise, but they only work if we use the stated conditions. We’re not allowed to invent better ones.”"},{"p":"“Rude,” Rafe said. “I had planned to invent a tailwind.”"},{"p":"We divided the work. I took checkpoints and route choices. Imani handled the weather packet. Beckett checked distances, estimated times, and the fuel arithmetic. Rafe made a one-page summary so somebody who hadn’t lived with the project for a week could follow our reasoning."},{"p":"For fifteen minutes, pencils scratched, pages turned, and Beckett complained quietly about numbers that refused to be round."},{"p":"Then Imani said, “We need to stop.”"},{"p":"I looked up. “What’s wrong?”"},{"p":"“The report for our first alternate is older than the one for the destination. The packet says a new observation is expected before our proposed departure, but we don’t have it.”"},{"p":"“Could still list it as an option,” I said."},{"p":"“As a possibility,” she replied. “Not as a confirmed suitable alternate.”"},{"p":"Beckett turned his worksheet sideways. “I already ran the numbers for that airport.”"},{"p":"“Which doesn’t make the weather better,” Imani said."},{"p":"“I didn’t say it did.”"},{"p":"Rafe raised one finger. “Before we begin the traditional festival of saying things nobody said, I have a question. Is there a deadline?”"},{"p":"There was. Mara wanted the completed briefing in twenty minutes. A group of younger academy students would arrive afterward to watch us present it."},{"p":"Beckett tapped the table. “If we switch alternates now, I need to redo the time and fuel estimates.”"},{"p":"“And if we don’t,” Imani said, “we’re presenting a plan that depends on information we know is incomplete.”"},{"p":"I felt the old urge rise in my chest. We were almost finished. We had a workable route. I wanted to call the first alternate good enough and move on."},{"p":"I opened my mouth, then saw Imani’s pencil resting flat across her notebook. She wasn’t being stubborn. She’d found a problem, and she was waiting to see whether we would treat it like one."},{"p":"“Let’s mark it conditional,” I said. “We can show both alternatives and explain what information would change the decision.”"},{"p":"Beckett frowned. “That means two sets of numbers.”"},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“We have nineteen minutes.”"},{"p":"“Also yeah.”"},{"p":"He stared at me for a moment, then drew a line down the center of his page. “Fine. But nobody is allowed to call these estimates ‘quick math.’”"},{"p":"Rafe wrote CONDITIONAL in enormous letters across the top of his summary."},{"p":"“Maybe slightly less enormous,” I suggested."},{"p":"“I’m communicating urgency.”"},{"p":"“You’re communicating that your marker is dying.”"},{"p":"We got to work. I checked the second alternate against the exercise’s airport information. Imani wrote down exactly which weather update we would need before deciding whether the first one was acceptable. Beckett recalculated the two options, calling out each assumption so we could catch a mistake before it traveled onto the poster. Rafe reorganized the briefing into three questions: Where are we going? What might change? What will we do if it does?"},{"p":"With six minutes left, Mara came in."},{"p":"“Status?” she asked."},{"p":"“Two plans,” Beckett said, “and one deeply unhappy calculator.”"},{"p":"Mara scanned the papers. “Why two?”"},{"p":"Imani explained the older weather observation. I described the choice to leave the alternate conditional. Beckett showed how the different routing affected his calculations."},{"p":"Mara nodded. “And what happens in a real preflight if the information you need hasn’t arrived?”"},{"p":"“We don’t pretend it has,” I said. “We get the update and reassess. If we still can’t verify the plan, we don’t depart just because the worksheet is due.”"},{"p":"“Good. A planning worksheet is not permission to fly.”"},{"p":"She looked at Rafe’s summary. “This is clear.”"},{"p":"Rafe pressed a hand to his chest. “I would like that in writing.”"},{"p":"“You’ve already written it in letters the size of runway markings.”"},{"p":"When the younger students arrived, we gave the briefing without pretending we’d reached one magical answer. We explained the missing observation and showed how a new report might change the choice. A boy in a green sweatshirt asked why we couldn’t just wait for the weather update before doing the assignment."},{"p":"“That’s actually one of the options,” I said."},{"p":"He shrugged. “Seems easier.”"},{"p":"“It might be,” I admitted."},{"p":"Afterward, the four of us stayed behind to clean up. Beckett was putting away the markers when he said, “I thought we were going to blow it.”"},{"p":"“Me too,” I said."},{"p":"Imani folded the chart. “You were about to tell us to keep the first alternate.”"},{"p":"I glanced at her. “I was.”"},{"p":"“What changed?”"},{"p":"I could have told her about leadership or teamwork or some other word that sounded impressive on a school banner. Instead I told the truth."},{"p":"“You sounded worried, and I finally figured out that was information.”"},{"p":"She gave me a small smile. It was small, but she didn’t look away."},{"p":"Rafe hoisted the poster tube like a telescope. “I see our future. It contains a snack machine.”"},{"p":"“Your predictions are remarkably consistent,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“And frequently correct.”"},{"p":"We went down the hall together, four people who still disagreed about plenty of things, including whether the vending machine’s cheese crackers qualified as food."},{"p":"Beckett and Rafe were still arguing about the cheese crackers when we reached the vending machine."}]},{"t":"Chapter Eighteen — The Letter on Mara’s Desk","blocks":[{"p":"On Friday afternoon, Dad came home carrying a grocery bag, two library books, and the expression he wore when he was trying not to announce something."},{"p":"“What?” I asked."},{"p":"He set the bag on the counter. “Hello to you, too.”"},{"p":"“Hi, Dad. What?”"},{"p":"“Have you ever considered that not everything is about you?”"},{"p":"“Sure. But you’re holding the bag like it contains a surprise.”"},{"p":"“It contains carrots.”"},{"p":"I looked inside. Carrots. Milk. A box of cereal with a cartoon tiger on it."},{"p":"“Suspicious,” I said."},{"p":"Dad grinned. “Mara called. She asked whether you could come by the academy tomorrow morning with a parent. Something about a meeting.”"},{"p":"My stomach tightened. “A meeting about what?”"},{"p":"“She said she’d explain there.”"},{"p":"“Did I do something?”"},{"p":"“Have you?”"},{"p":"“Not that I know of.”"},{"p":"“Comforting answer.”"},{"p":"I spent the evening constructing increasingly ridiculous explanations. Maybe Mara had discovered that I’d left a ruler in the classroom. Maybe my navigation worksheet had been entered in a competition without my knowledge. Maybe Rafe’s poster had somehow been classified as a billboard and the academy owed the county a permit fee."},{"p":"Mom found me at the kitchen table, staring at my phone."},{"p":"“You could just wait until tomorrow,” she said."},{"p":"“I’ve considered it. Terrible strategy.”"},{"p":"“Works surprisingly often.”"},{"p":"“Not for me.”"},{"p":"She pulled out the chair across from mine. “Whatever it is, you’ll hear it with us there.”"},{"p":"I looked at her. “Do you know?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Would you tell me if you did?”"},{"p":"“Depends. Are you going to interrogate me with a flashlight?”"},{"p":"“I could find one.”"},{"p":"“Then definitely not.”"},{"p":"Saturday morning arrived gray and breezy, with low clouds drifting inland from the coast. I checked the weather twice anyway, as if the second look might persuade the clouds to move."},{"p":"At the academy, Mara met us in the small briefing room. She had a folder on the table and a mug that said I’D RATHER BE FLYING, which seemed an unusual admission for someone who spent so much time telling people when not to."},{"p":"“Lincoln,” she said, “before we begin, I want to be clear that this isn’t a reward for being the loudest person in the room.”"},{"p":"“That eliminates several theories.”"},{"p":"Dad sat beside me. Mom took the chair nearest the window."},{"p":"Mara opened the folder. “The academy has been invited to nominate a small group of students for a regional youth aviation navigation program. It’s a weekend workshop series, not a substitute for flight training or a shortcut to a pilot certificate. Students work with instructors on flight planning, chart reading, communications, and supervised scenario exercises.”"},{"p":"I sat straighter."},{"p":"“Where?” I asked."},{"p":"“Some sessions are held here in Hampton Roads. Others may take place at a partner facility, depending on the final schedule. Travel arrangements would need to be worked out by families.”"},{"p":"She slid a printed letter toward us. Across the top were the words YOUTH NAVIGATION FELLOWSHIP — NOMINATION INVITATION."},{"p":"“The program coordinator asked for students who show they can prepare carefully, accept correction, and work with others,” Mara continued. “Your group project was one piece of evidence. Your training record is another. I’ve nominated you, along with several students from other academy cohorts.”"},{"p":"For a second I forgot how to speak."},{"p":"Dad rescued me. “What does being nominated mean?”"},{"p":"“An opportunity to apply,” Mara said. “Not acceptance. There’s an interview, a written exercise, and a family commitment form. We haven’t reviewed the final dates or costs. I wanted to talk with you before anyone assumed it was settled.”"},{"p":"Mom picked up the letter. “Would this interfere with school?”"},{"p":"“That’s one of the questions we’ll need answered. The organizers say they intend to schedule outside school hours, but we should verify that.”"},{"p":"“And his regular training?” Dad asked."},{"p":"“We’d adjust if necessary,” Mara said. “Lincoln’s flight instruction remains separate. This program does not authorize him to fly alone, and it doesn’t change the standards he’ll have to meet later.”"},{"p":"I barely heard the last sentence because one phrase was still ringing in my head."},{"p":"*I’ve nominated you.*"},{"p":"Mara looked at me. “You seem unusually quiet.”"},{"p":"“I’m trying not to grab the folder.”"},{"p":"“A wise instinct.”"},{"p":"“Why me?” I asked."},{"p":"She considered the question. “At the start of this year, when you had an answer, you wanted everyone to hear it immediately. Now you’re more willing to notice when somebody else has information you need. You still make mistakes. You just spend less time defending them.”"},{"p":"“That’s a very specific compliment.”"},{"p":"“It was meant to be.”"},{"p":"Dad leaned back, smiling at the ceiling. Mom kept reading the letter, already looking for the section that would explain transportation and fees."},{"p":"I thought of the four pencils around our map, the old weather report, and the boy who’d asked why we couldn’t simply wait. None of those moments had felt like an audition. Mostly they’d felt like trying to finish an assignment without upsetting my friends."},{"p":"“Can I tell the others?” I asked."},{"p":"“Tell them you’ve been nominated,” Mara said. “Don’t speculate about who else may be applying. That’s their news to share.”"},{"p":"I nodded. It was a small instruction, but I knew why she’d given it."},{"p":"Outside the briefing room, Mom stopped beside the vending machine while Dad went to bring the car around."},{"p":"“You want this,” she said."},{"p":"“So much.”"},{"p":"“Then we need to read everything carefully. Dates, costs, expectations. Wanting something doesn’t mean we skip the questions.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"She studied me. “You actually do, don’t you?”"},{"p":"“I’m learning.”"},{"p":"She squeezed my shoulder. “Good. Because your father is going to have approximately eight thousand questions.”"},{"p":"“He’s a librarian.”"},{"p":"“Exactly.”"},{"p":"In the car, I texted our group chat."},{"p":"*Mara nominated me for a regional youth navigation program. Still an application, not a sure thing. I’m kind of losing my mind.*"},{"p":"Rafe replied first."},{"p":"*Do they need a radio announcer? I have a voice.*"},{"p":"Beckett wrote: *Congratulations. Get the actual schedule before you commit.*"},{"p":"Imani’s message arrived last."},{"p":"*You should apply. And read the whole packet.*"},{"p":"Then, a moment later:"},{"p":"*Nana June says don’t forget your tomato plant while you’re becoming famous.*"},{"p":"I laughed hard enough that Dad asked whether the program had already rejected me."},{"p":"At home, I spread the letter across my desk. The application deadline was only three weeks away. The interview would include a group exercise with students I’d never met. A note at the bottom said selected participants might be invited to a later supervised introductory flight experience, subject to separate instructor approval and weather."},{"p":"I read that line three times."},{"p":"Not because it promised me anything. It didn’t."},{"p":"Because for the first time, the next step in aviation wasn’t only something I’d imagined for myself. Mara had nominated me. Now I had to decide what to do with the invitation."},{"p":"I reached for my pencil, stopped, and pulled out a blank sheet instead."},{"p":"At the top I wrote: *Questions before answers.*"},{"p":"Then I started a list."}]},{"t":"Chapter Nineteen — The Empty Chair","blocks":[{"p":"By Monday, my fellowship application had acquired three paper clips, a yellow folder, and the urgency usually reserved for missing dogs and school cancellations."},{"p":"Dad had made a calendar. Mom had made a budget. I had made a list of questions so long it looked like I was preparing to cross-examine an astronaut."},{"p":"None of us had filled out the actual application."},{"p":"“You understand the deadline is in nineteen days,” Mom said at breakfast."},{"p":"“Eighteen and a half,” I told her."},{"p":"“That wasn’t a request for greater precision.”"},{"p":"Dad slid the calendar toward me. He’d marked the interview date with a blue square and written CHECK TRANSPORTATION in letters big enough to read from the moon."},{"p":"“There’s a parent information session on Thursday,” he said. “I can attend. You have the academy meeting.”"},{"p":"“I can miss the academy meeting.”"},{"p":"“No,” Dad said. “You can do your part while I do mine.”"},{"p":"I studied the calendar. I’d been picturing the fellowship as one shiny thing with my name on it. On the kitchen calendar it looked like four separate jobs and a transportation problem."},{"p":"“Thanks,” I said."},{"p":"Dad looked startled enough that I felt obligated to ruin the moment."},{"p":"“Try not to embarrass me in front of the other parents.”"},{"p":"“I’ll wear my ceremonial librarian cape.”"},{"p":"At school, Imani was waiting outside the science classroom with her notebook pressed against her chest. Rafe was explaining to Beckett why a paper airplane could technically qualify as a regional transportation network if it crossed the county line."},{"p":"“Technically,” Beckett said, “it would qualify as litter.”"},{"p":"I arrived with my folder under one arm."},{"p":"Rafe spotted it. “Ah. The international man of mystery.”"},{"p":"“It’s regional,” I said. “And there’s an interview.”"},{"p":"“You’ve mentioned.”"},{"p":"His voice had a little edge. Not much. Enough that I noticed."},{"p":"I almost told him about the practice questions I’d been writing, then stopped. He was looking past me at the classroom door."},{"p":"“Did you want to see the packet?” I asked."},{"p":"“No, thanks. I’m good.”"},{"p":"He went inside before I could ask anything else."},{"p":"Imani watched him go. “He’s had a rough morning.”"},{"p":"“Because of me?”"},{"p":"“I don’t know. You could ask him.”"},{"p":"It was the sort of answer I deserved, which didn’t make it especially enjoyable."},{"p":"At the academy that afternoon, Mara gave us a new group exercise: prepare a route briefing for an imaginary flight from a coastal airport to an inland destination. Mara gave us clearly labeled fictional airport information, charts, and weather reports. Each person had one job. Imani handled weather, Beckett fuel and performance assumptions, Rafe communications and airport information, and I handled the route and the final briefing."},{"p":"“Fifteen minutes,” Mara said. “Then I’ll ask questions. Your team is responsible for the whole plan, not just your own column.”"},{"p":"We moved the desks together."},{"p":"“Departure at ten?” I asked."},{"p":"“Check the forecast validity period,” Imani said."},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"She gave me a look. I checked it."},{"p":"Beckett drew a neat table of distances. Rafe opened the airport information sheet and underlined a frequency."},{"p":"“That’s for the tower at the destination,” he said. “There’s a separate approach frequency listed here.”"},{"p":"“Good catch,” I said."},{"p":"He didn’t look up."},{"p":"We had seven minutes left when Beckett asked Rafe to verify the airport’s published runway information against the fictional airport directory. Rafe had been staring at his phone, where a message lit up and disappeared before I could read it."},{"p":"“Rafe?” Beckett said."},{"p":"“It’s there. Page three.”"},{"p":"“Can you check the length?”"},{"p":"“Page three,” he repeated."},{"p":"Beckett flipped to it. “This is the old sheet. The packet says one of the pages has been superseded.”"},{"p":"Rafe pushed his chair back. “Then use the new one.”"},{"p":"“That’s what I’m asking you to find.”"},{"p":"“I’m not your secretary.”"},{"p":"The room went quiet around our table. Imani set down her pencil."},{"p":"“Okay,” I said, trying for the voice Mara used when something went wrong. “We can just—”"},{"p":"“Just what?” Rafe asked. “Give me another assignment? Tell me how grateful I should be to work on Lincoln’s team?”"},{"p":"I felt my face get hot. “That’s not what I said.”"},{"p":"“You didn’t have to.”"},{"p":"Mara looked over from the far side of the room. She didn’t intervene yet."},{"p":"I wanted to tell Rafe how unfair he was being. I wanted to point out that I’d spent two weeks making sure everybody had a voice on our projects. I wanted to list every time I’d thanked him."},{"p":"Instead I stared at the page where he’d drawn a tiny headset on the margin, complete with an enormous microphone."},{"p":"“Did I make you feel like that?” I asked."},{"p":"He rubbed a hand across his forehead. “I don’t know.”"},{"p":"“That’s okay.”"},{"p":"“No, it isn’t.” His voice dropped. “You get nominated for something and everybody acts like it’s proof you’re the only one who ever worked hard. I know that’s not what Mara said. But it’s what it feels like.”"},{"p":"Imani glanced at me, then back at Rafe. She didn’t rescue either of us."},{"p":"“I didn’t know,” I said."},{"p":"“Right.”"},{"p":"“I didn’t ask.”"},{"p":"He looked at me then, properly. “No.”"},{"p":"Mara called, “Two minutes.”"},{"p":"The old version of me would have rushed to finish the presentation. I could practically hear him making a speech about how we needed to be professional."},{"p":"I closed my notebook."},{"p":"“We can’t brief an airport with information we haven’t verified,” I said. “Let’s tell Mara what we have and what we don’t.”"},{"p":"Beckett tapped his pencil. “We can still show the calculations as conditional.”"},{"p":"“Right,” I said. “And mark the airport selection unresolved.”"},{"p":"Rafe hesitated. Then he picked up the updated directory sheet from underneath a stack of unused maps."},{"p":"“Here,” he said. “I missed it.”"},{"p":"“We’ve all missed things,” I said."},{"p":"“Please don’t make that a motto.”"},{"p":"“Noted.”"},{"p":"We had time to correct the airport information, but not to check every related assumption. At the front of the room, I told Mara exactly that."},{"p":"She listened to our briefing, asked which information we would still verify before an actual departure, and nodded when Imani listed the outstanding weather questions."},{"p":"“Would you launch this flight based on the work you’ve done?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“No,” I said. “Not yet.”"},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"“Because the briefing isn’t finished. We found a problem late and we haven’t checked everything it affects.”"},{"p":"“Good,” she said. “A classroom deadline doesn’t make missing checks disappear.”"},{"p":"I expected her to ask why we were behind. She didn’t. She asked what our team would do differently next time."},{"p":"“Verify the source pages at the beginning,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Speak up when somebody’s distracted,” Imani added."},{"p":"Rafe looked at the floor. “And say what’s actually bothering you instead of picking a fight about page numbers.”"},{"p":"Mara’s eyebrows rose slightly. “That sounds like a useful lesson in several settings.”"},{"p":"Afterward, Rafe and I ended up outside by the vending machines. The sky had cleared into a bright blue that made the puddles on the pavement look like pieces of it had fallen off."},{"p":"“Sorry,” he said."},{"p":"“Me too.”"},{"p":"“You didn’t do anything wrong by getting nominated.”"},{"p":"“I know. But I was talking about it a lot.”"},{"p":"“A lot,” he agreed."},{"p":"“How much is a lot?”"},{"p":"“If you mentioned the fellowship one more time, I was going to nominate you for a fellowship in not mentioning the fellowship.”"},{"p":"I laughed. So did he, eventually."},{"p":"He leaned against the wall. “I applied for the youth radio club at school. They picked somebody else for the announcing spot. I didn’t tell you guys.”"},{"p":"“That stinks.”"},{"p":"“Yeah. And then you had this thing. I was happy for you and also not happy for me. Turns out those feelings can occupy the same head.”"},{"p":"“Mine has room for at least six bad decisions at once.”"},{"p":"“I’ve noticed.”"},{"p":"I waited while he scuffed his shoe against the concrete."},{"p":"“Would you want help practicing the interview?” he asked. “I’m pretty good at making people nervous with questions.”"},{"p":"“That’s a terrifying offer.”"},{"p":"“It’s a gift.”"},{"p":"I thought about the folder in my backpack and the blank answer I’d left on the page labeled TEAMWORK."},{"p":"“Yeah,” I said. “I would.”"},{"p":"When Mom picked me up, I didn’t tell her our team had worked perfectly. It hadn’t. I told her we’d argued, repaired what we could, and given an honest briefing."},{"p":"She listened until I finished."},{"p":"“Sounds like a busy fifteen minutes,” she said."},{"p":"“Fifteen minutes and possibly three years of emotional development.”"},{"p":"“Your father will want to put that on the calendar.”"},{"p":"I watched the academy disappear in the side mirror. Rafe still had reasons to be disappointed. But while Mom waited at the next red light, my phone lit up with a message from him."},{"p":"*Interview practice. Wednesday. Bring your best terrible answers.*"},{"p":"For once, I had no trouble admitting I needed help."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty — The Question They Didn’t Ask","blocks":[{"p":"On interview morning, I wore a button-down shirt Mom had ironed and shoes that made a noise on the kitchen floor like somebody knocking politely on a door."},{"p":"Dad watched me pace between the refrigerator and the table."},{"p":"“You’re going to wear a trench in the tile,” he said."},{"p":"“Good. We can use it to measure my groundspeed.”"},{"p":"“Not without a reliable clock.”"},{"p":"He handed me a banana. I held it without eating it."},{"p":"The fellowship interviews were held in a conference room at a local aviation education center. The program coordinator, Ms. Vale, greeted us at a table with a sign-in sheet and a pitcher of water. She explained that the program was a regional youth enrichment initiative, with workshops run by participating instructors and volunteers. Selection wouldn’t count as flight instruction or guarantee a chance to fly."},{"p":"Mom asked about the fees. Ms. Vale showed her the scholarship and transportation information. Dad asked about supervision. She showed him the parent handbook."},{"p":"I asked where the bathroom was."},{"p":"At least I had one question I could answer."},{"p":"My first interview was with Ms. Vale and another instructor, Mr. Kline, whose hair was almost completely silver and whose expression suggested he’d never laughed at a bad aviation joke in his life."},{"p":"I sat across from them with my hands on my knees."},{"p":"“Why do you want to join?” Ms. Vale asked."},{"p":"I’d practiced this answer with Rafe four times. Each time, he’d pretended to fall asleep halfway through."},{"p":"“Because I want to become a better pilot someday,” I said. “But mostly because I want to learn how to make decisions when the answer isn’t obvious. I like maps. I like figuring things out. I don’t always like being wrong, which is probably why I need the practice.”"},{"p":"Mr. Kline wrote something down."},{"p":"“Tell us about a time you disagreed with a teammate.”"},{"p":"I thought of the classroom, Rafe’s face, and the outdated airport page."},{"p":"“We had an assignment where I wanted to finish fast,” I said. “One teammate was frustrated about something I hadn’t noticed. We argued. The plan wasn’t ready when time ran out.”"},{"p":"“What did you do?”"},{"p":"“We told our instructor what wasn’t verified. We didn’t pretend the plan was complete.”"},{"p":"“Was that a success?” he asked."},{"p":"“Not the way I wanted. But we didn’t turn an unfinished plan into a fake finished one.”"},{"p":"Mr. Kline’s pen stopped moving."},{"p":"“What would you change?”"},{"p":"“I’d ask earlier if everyone had what they needed. And I’d notice when somebody was having a hard day instead of assuming they’re being difficult.”"},{"p":"Ms. Vale smiled a little. “Thank you.”"},{"p":"Then came the written exercise. We were given a simplified, clearly labeled training chart, a list of hypothetical airports, and a scenario about a family planning a daytime flight. The assignment asked us to identify information we’d need before recommending a route. There wasn’t enough information to approve a real flight, and the instructions told us not to guess."},{"p":"I wrote down weather briefings, aircraft performance, fuel requirements and reserves, current airport information, airspace, terrain, passenger considerations, and alternatives. I underlined *current* twice."},{"p":"When I finished, I checked my answers and discovered I’d written the wrong date at the top."},{"p":"I corrected it without attempting to explain the mistake to the paper."},{"p":"The last part was a group exercise with three other applicants. One was a girl named Priya who spoke quickly and had a ruler tucked behind her ear. Another was a boy named Nico who asked careful questions before touching his pencil. The third was a tall kid named Ellis who arrived with a notebook full of tabs."},{"p":"Our task was to design a navigation workshop for younger students using a fictional route and paper checkpoints. We had twenty minutes to prepare a five-minute demonstration."},{"p":"Priya immediately started sketching a map."},{"p":"“Let’s make it a treasure hunt,” she said."},{"p":"“Could be confusing if the landmarks look alike,” Nico said."},{"p":"“That’s the challenge,” Ellis replied."},{"p":"I pictured a giant compass rose on the floor. Everyone would be impressed. Probably."},{"p":"Then I remembered four pencils around a map."},{"p":"“What do we want the kids to learn?” I asked."},{"p":"Priya looked up. “How to find a place?”"},{"p":"“Maybe how to check whether they’ve found the right place,” Nico said."},{"p":"Ellis nodded. “We could give them two similar landmarks.”"},{"p":"“And make the directions depend on more than one clue,” I said."},{"p":"We built the exercise together. Priya drew the landmarks. Nico made the question cards. Ellis checked that the clues matched the map. I wrote the instructions and tried not to write a speech."},{"p":"Halfway through, Priya noticed that one card sent the students to a lake while the map showed two lakes."},{"p":"“Which one?” she asked."},{"p":"I almost answered immediately. Then I leaned over the drawing."},{"p":"“Good question,” I said. “We need another clue.”"},{"p":"She added a road bend beside one lake and smiled as though she’d just hidden a secret passage."},{"p":"When the evaluators arrived, our demonstration went well until Mr. Kline picked up the final card and said, “Suppose the road bend isn’t visible.”"},{"p":"My stomach dropped. We hadn’t planned for that."},{"p":"Priya glanced at me. Ellis’s pencil stopped. Nico opened his mouth, then closed it."},{"p":"I could feel the old urge to improvise something impressive."},{"p":"Instead I said, “Then the students shouldn’t claim they know which lake it is. We’d want them to stop and use another reference or ask for help.”"},{"p":"Mr. Kline looked at the others. “Do you agree?”"},{"p":"Nico nodded. Priya pointed to a hill on the map. “We could add this as an alternate clue, if the students can identify it.”"},{"p":"“And if they can’t,” Ellis said, “the correct answer is that they don’t have enough information.”"},{"p":"Mr. Kline made another note. For a moment I thought he might smile. He didn’t, but his face became less like a locked filing cabinet."},{"p":"Afterward, I found Mom and Dad in the lobby. Dad had finished a crossword puzzle. Mom was comparing transportation schedules with another parent."},{"p":"“How’d it go?” she asked."},{"p":"“I don’t know.”"},{"p":"Dad folded the newspaper. “That sounds more believable than perfect.”"},{"p":"“I answered everything I could. Some questions were harder than I expected.”"},{"p":"“Good,” Mom said. “That’s what an interview should do.”"},{"p":"The acceptance email arrived eleven days later while I was helping Dad carry a stack of donated books into the library’s storage room. My phone buzzed against my leg. I pulled it out with dusty fingers and saw the subject line."},{"p":"YOUTH NAVIGATION FELLOWSHIP — SELECTION RESULT."},{"p":"“Dad.”"},{"p":"He set down the box. “What?”"},{"p":"I opened the message and read it once, then again because the first reading didn’t seem trustworthy."},{"p":"*We are pleased to offer you a place in the upcoming cohort, contingent upon completion of the family paperwork and orientation requirements.*"},{"p":"I handed him the phone."},{"p":"He read it. His smile started slowly, then took over his whole face."},{"p":"“Well,” he said. “Look at that.”"},{"p":"“I got in.”"},{"p":"“You did.”"},{"p":"I wanted to jump. I wanted to shout. Instead I stood in the library storage room holding an empty cardboard box while Dad put his hand on the back of my neck."},{"p":"“Proud of you,” he said."},{"p":"That did it. I grinned so hard my cheeks hurt."},{"p":"At home, Mom hugged me before asking about the paperwork. Imani sent a string of airplane emojis followed by *Read the orientation packet.* Beckett asked whether the schedule included travel reimbursement. Rafe called and opened with a dramatic announcer voice."},{"p":"“Ladies and gentlemen, the regional fellowship has made a questionable but entertaining decision.”"},{"p":"“Thank you,” I said."},{"p":"“I’m serious. Congratulations.”"},{"p":"“Thanks for practicing with me.”"},{"p":"“Don’t get sentimental. I’m charging by the question next time.”"},{"p":"That night I printed the acceptance letter and placed it beside the original nomination. Between them lay the interview worksheet, covered in corrections and pencil marks."},{"p":"Another email arrived with the preliminary orientation agenda. One line caught my eye:"},{"p":"*All participants will complete an individual readiness evaluation with an assigned program instructor before joining advanced scenario sessions.*"},{"p":"I read it twice. Then I looked at the name listed below it."},{"p":"**Evaluator: Captain Rowan Pike, Flight Instructor.**"},{"p":"I didn’t know Captain Pike. I didn’t know what the evaluation involved. The message said details would follow after the parent orientation."},{"p":"From the hallway, Dad called, “Everything okay?”"},{"p":"“Yeah,” I said. “I think so.”"},{"p":"I looked at the acceptance letter again. Getting in had felt like reaching the top of something."},{"p":"Apparently there was another staircase."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty-One — The Frequency Nobody Owned","blocks":[{"p":"The first thing Rafe said when I told him about Captain Rowan Pike was, “That sounds like a man who checks his cereal for loose screws.”"},{"p":"We were sitting at the picnic table behind the academy, where the sun had warmed the wooden benches enough to make them uncomfortable. Beckett had spread three pages of aircraft performance notes across the table and was using his water bottle to keep them from blowing into the grass. Imani had a notebook open but wasn’t writing in it."},{"p":"“You’ve never met him,” I said."},{"p":"“Neither have you. That’s what makes this a fair competition.”"},{"p":"“Competition for what?” Beckett asked."},{"p":"“Most accurate completely unsupported description.”"},{"p":"Imani turned a page. “I think you should wait until you meet him before deciding he’s a cereal inspector.”"},{"p":"“Fine,” Rafe said. “He might be a toast inspector.”"},{"p":"I laughed, then caught myself checking the orientation email again. It had been three days since the acceptance letter. Three days in which the words *individual readiness evaluation* had somehow grown heavier each time I read them."},{"p":"Mara came through the back door carrying a stack of laminated cards."},{"p":"“Anyone interested in doing something useful?” she asked."},{"p":"Beckett lifted his performance notes. “I’m already doing something useful.”"},{"p":"“Then you’ll be pleased to know I need your help.”"},{"p":"She set the cards down. Each showed a fictional route, a weather summary, and a few airport details. One corner of every card had a small drawing of a radio."},{"p":"“Saturday’s junior workshop needs a communications station,” she said. “The younger students are going to practice what information belongs in a position report and how to ask for help when they need it. No transmitting on an actual aviation frequency. This is a tabletop exercise.”"},{"p":"Rafe sat up. “I volunteer as tribute.”"},{"p":"“You volunteer to help design it,” Mara corrected. “The whole group does. I want the children to leave knowing they can speak clearly without pretending they know something they don’t.”"},{"p":"She walked back inside before we could ask whether she had invented that assignment specifically for us."},{"p":"Rafe picked up a card. “I call the radio station.”"},{"p":"“You can’t call an entire station,” Beckett said. “We haven’t even decided what it is.”"},{"p":"“Then I call the part that talks.”"},{"p":"I reached for a pencil. “We should probably map out the whole exercise first.”"},{"p":"Rafe put the card down. “Can I have one thing?”"},{"p":"He didn’t say it loudly. That was what made the question land so hard."},{"p":"The others went still. I looked at the pencil in my hand, at the blank paper, and then at him."},{"p":"“I wasn’t trying to take it,” I said."},{"p":"“I know.” He rubbed his forehead. “You get the fellowship, and then every project somehow becomes practice for your fellowship. I just wanted to do the radio part.”"},{"p":"My face went hot. The last time we’d argued, I’d told myself I would listen sooner. Apparently *sooner* was still a skill I needed to practice."},{"p":"“Okay,” I said. “You design the communications station. Tell us what you need.”"},{"p":"Rafe eyed me. “You’re not going to make a flowchart?”"},{"p":"“I might draw a flowchart privately and never show anybody.”"},{"p":"“That’s disturbing, but acceptable.”"},{"p":"He took a sheet of paper and began writing. Within minutes he had a pretend ground station, a student pilot who wasn’t sure of a checkpoint, and a script with a deliberately confusing first call."},{"p":"Beckett tested the script by reading the student part in the voice of a pirate. Imani told him pirates would probably have more confidence than that. Rafe nearly fell off the bench laughing."},{"p":"Then we hit a problem."},{"p":"On Rafe’s third card, the student pilot said they were east of a reservoir. On the map, the reservoir had two arms, and the checkpoint description didn’t establish which side of the water the student meant. The ground station’s scripted response gave a heading as though the location were certain."},{"p":"“That’s not okay,” Imani said."},{"p":"Rafe looked down at the card. “It’s a pretend exercise.”"},{"p":"“It’s teaching them what to say when they’re unsure,” she answered. “We shouldn’t teach them to turn a guess into a location.”"},{"p":"Beckett tapped the map. “The ground station can’t figure out where the airplane is from that sentence.”"},{"p":"Rafe’s jaw tightened. I recognized the expression. It was the one I’d worn when someone pointed out a problem with a plan I had already decided was good."},{"p":"“It’s one card,” he said."},{"p":"“Then it’s one card we can fix,” I said."},{"p":"He looked at me sharply, perhaps expecting me to take over."},{"p":"I slid the pencil toward him instead."},{"p":"For a few seconds he didn’t touch it. Then he drew a line through the heading in the scripted reply."},{"p":"“What if the station asks for another landmark?” he said. “And if they still can’t place themselves, the student says that instead of making up a better answer.”"},{"p":"“Good,” Imani said."},{"p":"Rafe added a note: *Don’t give a position you cannot establish.*"},{"p":"I looked at it and thought of the flight with Mara when I’d wanted to name a town because it felt embarrassing not to. I wondered how many different ways a person could learn the same lesson before it finally stayed learned."},{"p":"On Saturday, the workshop ran in a classroom with masking-tape runways on the floor. Rafe wore an old headset that wasn’t connected to anything. He had written GROUND STATION on a piece of cardboard and taped it to the table."},{"p":"A boy in a dinosaur shirt approached with a route card and read his first line so quietly we could barely hear him."},{"p":"Rafe leaned forward. “Want to try that again? You don’t have to sound like a movie pilot. Just tell me what you know.”"},{"p":"The boy tried again. This time he named his pretend aircraft, described his uncertainty, and asked for help identifying the checkpoint."},{"p":"Rafe didn’t rush him. He asked a question about the road on the map. The boy found it, then found the water tower beside it. His whole face brightened."},{"p":"“There!” he said. “I know where I am.”"},{"p":"“You have two matching clues,” Rafe said. “That’s a much better answer.”"},{"p":"I watched from the far side of the room, where Imani was helping a girl sort time-stamped weather cards. Beckett had turned a cardboard box into a very serious-looking fuel-planning desk."},{"p":"Rafe wasn’t pretending to be an air traffic controller. He wasn’t teaching children to use a real radio without an instructor. He was showing them how to say the thing that was hard to admit: *I need more information.*"},{"p":"He caught me watching and pointed at his cardboard sign."},{"p":"“Official station business,” he said. “Move along.”"},{"p":"After the workshop, Mara collected the cards and told us she’d heard from a volunteer who helped run a local aviation communications club. They were looking for a youth assistant to help with supervised demonstrations of aviation phraseology, listening skills, and emergency communication procedures at community events. No actual controlling, no unsupervised transmissions, and no promise of a permanent position."},{"p":"Rafe blinked. “Seriously?”"},{"p":"“I told them you’d be worth meeting,” Mara said. “If you’re interested, I’ll introduce you and your parents.”"},{"p":"He stared at her for a moment, then at the cardboard sign still hanging from the table."},{"p":"“Do I have to bring this?”"},{"p":"“I would strongly advise against it,” she said."},{"p":"He grinned anyway."},{"p":"Walking to the parking lot, Rafe bumped my shoulder with his."},{"p":"“I’m going to apply,” he said."},{"p":"“You should.”"},{"p":"“And if I get it, I’m going to mention it constantly.”"},{"p":"“Fair.”"},{"p":"“Possibly during your wedding.”"},{"p":"“That’s a long way to travel for a joke.”"},{"p":"“I believe in planning ahead.”"},{"p":"At home, I told Dad about Rafe’s opportunity before I told him anything about my own evaluation."},{"p":"He listened while he made sandwiches at the kitchen counter. When I finished, he set a plate in front of me."},{"p":"“Sounds like he found something that fits him.”"},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"Dad reached for the mustard. “That’s good news.”"},{"p":"It was. I didn’t have to compare it to my news to know that."},{"p":"Later, when I opened the fellowship portal, a new document waited under the orientation tab. The title was plain enough:"},{"p":"*Readiness Evaluation — Participant Information.*"},{"p":"I clicked it. The first page listed chart reading, route planning, weather interpretation, and decision-making under changing conditions. Nothing I hadn’t studied. Then I reached the sentence at the bottom."},{"p":"*Participants may be asked to explain why a proposed plan should not proceed.*"},{"p":"I read that line again."},{"p":"I had spent most of the year learning how to build a plan. Apparently the next step was proving I could set one aside."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty-Two — The Red Pencil","blocks":[{"p":"Captain Rowan Pike didn’t look like a cereal inspector."},{"p":"He looked like the sort of person who would notice that I’d made that joke and ask me to explain the evidence supporting it."},{"p":"He was standing beside a conference table when Mom and I arrived for the fellowship readiness evaluation. He had close-cropped gray hair, a plain blue shirt, and a clipboard with a bent corner. No flight jacket. No dramatic entrance. His name tag said ROWAN PIKE, CFI."},{"p":"“Lincoln Burgess?” he asked."},{"p":"“Yes, sir.”"},{"p":"“I’m Rowan Pike. You can call me Captain Pike or Mr. Pike. Your choice.”"},{"p":"Mom introduced herself. He shook her hand, showed her the parent waiting area, and explained that the evaluation was a ground-based planning and judgment exercise. It would not authorize any flight, replace Mara’s instruction, or determine whether I was ready to solo."},{"p":"I appreciated the explanation right up until he added, “I don’t grade confidence. I grade the reasoning you can show me.”"},{"p":"I suddenly wished I’d brought a larger supply of reasoning."},{"p":"The table held a sectional-chart excerpt marked TRAINING SCENARIO, a current-for-the-exercise airport information sheet, a weather packet, and a blank navigation log. Pike pointed to a chair."},{"p":"“You have thirty minutes to develop a proposed route between these two fictional training locations,” he said. “Use only the information provided. State what you’d need to verify before any real flight. I may update the scenario.”"},{"p":"I sat down. He handed me a red pencil."},{"p":"“Why red?” I asked."},{"p":"“It’s the one that was sharp.”"},{"p":"I almost laughed. He didn’t."},{"p":"The first fifteen minutes went well. I identified checkpoints that were distinct enough to recognize from the air, wrote estimated times using the supplied example groundspeed, and noted that the exercise’s fuel figures were assumptions rather than aircraft-specific operating data. I listed the information I’d need from an actual aircraft’s approved documents and a current briefing."},{"p":"Pike watched without hovering. Once, when I paused over the weather page, he asked what I was looking for."},{"p":"“Whether the forecast covers the time I’d actually be there,” I said."},{"p":"“Continue.”"},{"p":"That was all."},{"p":"At twenty minutes he slid another page onto the table."},{"p":"*UPDATE: The planned destination’s forecast has deteriorated. An alternate field appears closer on the chart, but its published information in your packet is older than the rest of the material.*"},{"p":"I looked up. “Am I allowed to ask for more information?”"},{"p":"“You may ask. You may not receive it.”"},{"p":"I checked the alternate. The runway length in the old packet might have been adequate for the example aircraft under some conditions, but there was no basis for treating it as verified. I could imagine Beckett leaning over my shoulder, demanding performance figures. I could imagine Imani pointing at the date."},{"p":"I crossed out the alternate and wrote *VERIFY CURRENT DATA BEFORE USE.*"},{"p":"Pike glanced at the note. “So what’s your decision?”"},{"p":"“I wouldn’t approve this plan as it stands.”"},{"p":"“Would you continue toward the original destination?”"},{"p":"“Not on the information here.”"},{"p":"“Would you divert to the closer airport?”"},{"p":"“Not until I knew it was suitable. And in a real airplane, we’d be working with the instructor, the current conditions, the fuel we actually had, and the options available. This worksheet doesn’t tell me enough to pick a safe destination.”"},{"p":"He nodded once. “You’ve identified uncertainty. What do you do with it?”"},{"p":"I thought of Mara’s classroom. The sealed envelope. The day we stayed on the ground while thunder moved across the field. Rafe’s cardboard radio station."},{"p":"“I stop pretending the original plan is still complete,” I said. “I find out what I can, and if the information isn’t available before departure, I don’t go.”"},{"p":"Pike folded his arms. “Reasonable. But your assignment was to produce a route. You haven’t finished it.”"},{"p":"My stomach tightened."},{"p":"“No,” I said."},{"p":"“Does that bother you?”"},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"“Because I wanted to do well.”"},{"p":"“And do you think finishing the form would mean you did well?”"},{"p":"I looked at the red pencil. I’d filled in almost every box. The empty destination line looked accusing."},{"p":"“Not if I filled it with something I couldn’t support.”"},{"p":"For the first time, the corner of his mouth moved. It might have been a smile, or a muscle remembering how."},{"p":"He collected the papers and asked me to wait outside while he reviewed them. Mom was reading a magazine that looked older than the building."},{"p":"“How was it?” she asked."},{"p":"“I didn’t finish.”"},{"p":"She put the magazine down. “What happened?”"},{"p":"I told her about the updated weather and the airport information I couldn’t verify. She listened without interrupting."},{"p":"“Do you think you made the right decision?” she asked."},{"p":"“I think so.”"},{"p":"“Then that’s worth something.”"},{"p":"I nodded, but my hands were still shaking a little. I wanted the kind of result you could frame, not the kind you had to explain."},{"p":"When Pike called us back in, he had two sheets on his clipboard."},{"p":"“You demonstrated sound instincts about incomplete information,” he said. “You also identified several planning assumptions clearly. Those are strengths.”"},{"p":"I felt myself straighten."},{"p":"“However,” he continued, “you missed an important item in the original packet.”"},{"p":"He turned the chart toward me and tapped a small note beside one of my proposed checkpoints. A temporary restriction in the fictional exercise had been marked on the accompanying notice sheet. I’d read the sheet. I’d even underlined its date. But I’d failed to connect the notice to the route I drew."},{"p":"I stared at the mark. It was there. Small, but there."},{"p":"“I missed that,” I said."},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"“I should have checked the route against the notices before I called it usable.”"},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"He didn’t make the mistake larger than it was. Somehow that made it harder to shrug off."},{"p":"Pike placed the second sheet in front of me. Across the top was my name. Below it were boxes marked *communication*, *weather judgment*, *route verification*, and *response to changing conditions*."},{"p":"Three boxes carried encouraging comments. One was marked in red."},{"p":"**RESULT: CONDITIONAL — ROUTE VERIFICATION REASSESSMENT REQUIRED.**"},{"p":"I read the words twice."},{"p":"“Does this mean I’m out of the fellowship?”"},{"p":"“No,” Pike said. “You’re accepted. This means you don’t advance to the next planning scenario until you demonstrate a reliable verification method. You have time to prepare. The reassessment will be conducted under the program’s procedures, with your parents informed.”"},{"p":"I wanted to say that I knew how to verify a route. I wanted to explain all the other times I’d done it correctly. I could almost hear the words lining up, ready to march out and defend me."},{"p":"Then I looked at the note I’d missed."},{"p":"“What should I practice?” I asked."},{"p":"Pike handed me a checklist template with the boxes left blank."},{"p":"“Build a method you can explain and repeat. Don’t build a performance for me.”"},{"p":"Outside, the parking lot was full of afternoon sunlight. Mom unlocked the car and waited while I stood beside the passenger door."},{"p":"“You’re quiet,” she said."},{"p":"“I got a conditional result.”"},{"p":"“I heard.”"},{"p":"“I thought I was ready.”"},{"p":"“Ready for what?”"},{"p":"I didn’t answer immediately. Ready to be impressive, maybe. Ready for somebody new to tell me I’d learned enough. Ready to stop being the kid who needed someone to check his work."},{"p":"“I don’t know,” I admitted."},{"p":"Mom opened her door. “You can be proud of getting into the program and disappointed about today. Those things can both fit in one car.”"},{"p":"I looked across the roof at her. “Did Dad tell you to say that?”"},{"p":"“No. Your father would have made it about a library card.”"},{"p":"I laughed despite myself."},{"p":"At home, I sent Mara a photograph of the evaluation sheet and asked when we could review the missed notice. She replied a few minutes later."},{"p":"*Tomorrow after school. Bring the whole packet. We’ll find out where your process broke down.*"},{"p":"Then another message arrived, this one from Rafe."},{"p":"*The communications club wants to meet me and my parents Thursday. I have been advised not to wear the cardboard sign.*"},{"p":"I typed *Congratulations* and meant it. A second later I added *Please wear the sign.*"},{"p":"His reply was a picture of the sign propped against his bedroom wall."},{"p":"I set my phone beside the red pencil Pike had let me keep. Its point was worn flat from all the lines I’d drawn and erased."},{"p":"On my desk sat the fellowship acceptance letter, the conditional evaluation, and a clean sheet of paper. I drew four columns: *source*, *date*, *route impact*, *verified?*"},{"p":"For the first time all day, I felt like I knew what to do next."},{"p":"Not because the evaluation had gone the way I’d hoped."},{"p":"Because it hadn’t."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty-Three — The Part We Could Prove","blocks":[{"p":"The next afternoon, Mara put my route on the table between us and handed me a transparent ruler."},{"p":"“Tell me where you stopped checking,” she said."},{"p":"I had prepared for a speech, so her useful question caught me off guard."},{"p":"“The notices,” I said. “I read them, but I didn’t compare every one with the route.”"},{"p":"“Show me.”"},{"p":"I laid the ruler along the line I’d drawn for Captain Pike. It looked almost beautiful: a straight pencil mark joining carefully chosen checkpoints. I had liked the line so much I treated the rest of the packet as somebody else’s problem."},{"p":"“Here’s the notice,” I said, sliding the page beside the chart. “I checked its date. Then I moved on.”"},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"“Because I’d already decided where I wanted to go.”"},{"p":"Mara waited. Her silences were harder than any question on the worksheet."},{"p":"“I wasn’t checking whether the route worked,” I said. “I was checking things off so I could say it worked.”"},{"p":"“That distinction matters.”"},{"p":"She didn’t take my pencil. Instead, she asked me to describe what each column on my new worksheet meant. *Source.* Where had the information come from? *Date.* Was it current for the planning exercise? *Route impact.* Did it change anything about the proposed flight? *Verified?* Had I actually checked the effect, or merely seen the page?"},{"p":"I’d filled in the first three columns at home. The last was mostly question marks."},{"p":"Mara leaned over them. “These are the most useful marks on the page.”"},{"p":"“That’s a terrible thing to tell a person who spent an hour making the other columns neat.”"},{"p":"“The neat columns are lovely.”"},{"p":"“You sound like Dad when he says my handwriting is ‘legible in favorable lighting.’”"},{"p":"She smiled. “Now. How would you check the route again?”"},{"p":"We went through the fictional planning packet slowly. I matched each applicable notice to the route, considered whether it changed the plan, and wrote down the unanswered questions. When a notice didn’t apply, I recorded why. When I couldn’t tell, I left it open."},{"p":"By the end, arrows crowded the route, two checkpoints were crossed out, and a box marked where I needed updated information before I could recommend anything."},{"p":"It wasn’t pretty anymore."},{"p":"Mara studied the page. “Would you send this to Captain Pike?”"},{"p":"“Not as a finished route.”"},{"p":"“As evidence of how you’re learning?”"},{"p":"I looked at the mess of pencil marks. “Maybe after I erase the part where I wrote ‘WHY IS EVERYTHING A FOOTNOTE?’”"},{"p":"“Keep that. It communicates enthusiasm.”"},{"p":"I laughed. Then I folded the packet and slid it into my backpack. The reassessment was still ahead of me, so I planned to practice at home."},{"p":"Outside the classroom, Beckett was balancing a cardboard runway on two chairs while Rafe explained to a younger student why a radio call should contain useful information rather than a detailed account of what he’d eaten for lunch."},{"p":"“Depends on the lunch,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Negative,” Rafe replied. “Nobody needs a full sandwich report on the frequency.”"},{"p":"Imani sat on the floor beside the display, writing labels in her tidy block letters. When she noticed me, she lifted her eyebrows toward my folder."},{"p":"“How’d it go?”"},{"p":"“I’m still conditional.”"},{"p":"“I asked how it went.”"},{"p":"“Better,” I admitted. “I know what I skipped.”"},{"p":"She nodded and moved her notebook aside to make room for me. I sat beside her."},{"p":"Rafe joined us carrying the cardboard radio, which had acquired a new antenna made from a drinking straw."},{"p":"“The communications club wants me to help with their youth demonstration next month,” he announced. “It’s not official until they talk to my parents, but they said I asked good questions.”"},{"p":"“Did you tell them about the sandwich?” I asked."},{"p":"“I am saving my best material for the interview.”"},{"p":"Beckett lowered the runway onto the chairs. “You’ll be famous for getting banned from a radio.”"},{"p":"“That’s still fame.”"},{"p":"Imani passed me a marker. “We’re doing one last planning board for the younger group. Two possible routes, one missing weather update. You want to write the question?”"},{"p":"I nearly wrote *Which route is best?* It would have been easier to grade."},{"p":"Instead I wrote: *What would you need to know before choosing?*"},{"p":"“That’s harder,” Beckett said, reading over my shoulder."},{"p":"“Yeah,” I said. “Leave it.”"},{"p":"The younger students arrived ten minutes later, bringing a remarkable amount of energy and one paper airplane that struck Rafe directly in the forehead. We helped them compare the fictional routes. One student wanted to choose the shortest line. Another pointed out that the weather information was old. A third asked whether the alternate airport had fuel."},{"p":"I started to answer, then caught myself."},{"p":"“What does the information sheet tell us?” I asked."},{"p":"They bent over it together. It didn’t tell them enough."},{"p":"“So we can’t pick?” the first student said, disappointed."},{"p":"“Not yet,” I said. “But you just figured out what to check next.”"},{"p":"She considered that, then wrote *GET NEW WEATHER* in letters large enough to be seen from the parking lot."},{"p":"“Good catch,” I said, and left the marker in her hand."},{"p":"By the time Dad came to collect me, the cardboard runway had collapsed, Rafe had found the paper airplane’s owner, and Imani had promised to send Nana June a picture of the group’s display. Nana June’s tomatoes were apparently growing with the confidence of plants that had never seen a forecast."},{"p":"Dad waited while I buckled my seat belt."},{"p":"“Good day?” he asked."},{"p":"“Good enough to be annoying.”"},{"p":"“Those are the educational ones.”"},{"p":"We turned out of the airport lot. The evening light lay across the road, and I watched the painted lane markings pass beneath us. When Dad slowed for a roadwork sign, I checked the detour arrow and kept my mouth shut."},{"p":"At home, a thick envelope was waiting on the kitchen counter. It carried the fellowship’s logo and my name in careful black letters. Underneath, in smaller print, were the words *Additional Program Information Enclosed*."},{"p":"I set my backpack down."},{"p":"Mom looked up from the stove. “We haven’t opened it.”"},{"p":"Dad came in behind me. “For the record, I showed enormous restraint.”"},{"p":"“You held it up to the lamp,” Mom said."},{"p":"“I was checking the envelope’s structural integrity.”"},{"p":"I slid a finger beneath the flap. Inside were a welcome letter, a calendar, and a page titled *Advanced Training Invitation*."},{"p":"The first paragraph made my heart race. The fellowship had arranged a weekend navigation lab with an aviation museum’s education team and a regional flight-training organization. Accepted students could apply to attend with a parent or guardian. The lab would include ground planning, a simulator session, and an instructor-supervised aircraft familiarization activity if weather, scheduling, and individual eligibility permitted. There was no solo flight in the program."},{"p":"I read it twice, then found the last page."},{"p":"*Students holding a conditional readiness result must complete the required reassessment before participating in the advanced navigation scenarios. Additional readiness screening may be required.*"},{"p":"There was a name beneath the notice."},{"p":"**Captain Rowan Pike — Evaluation Lead.**"},{"p":"Dad leaned over my shoulder. “That’s the guy with the red pencil?”"},{"p":"“That’s him.”"},{"p":"“Good stationery budget, apparently.”"},{"p":"I was thrilled—until I remembered the unfinished route in my backpack and Pike’s red box around the missed notice."},{"p":"Mom touched the edge of the letter. “You don’t have to decide tonight.”"},{"p":"I looked at the calendar. The application deadline was three weeks away. The reassessment date wasn’t on it."},{"p":"“I know,” I said."},{"p":"Then I picked up my pencil."},{"p":"Not to fill out the application. Not yet."},{"p":"I wrote one question on the back of the invitation: *What do I need to verify first?*"},{"p":"I left the application blank and put the question beside my route worksheet."},{"p":"For the first time, that question felt less like an obstacle and more like the beginning of somewhere I wanted to go."}]},{"t":"Epilogue — A Different Kind of Departure","blocks":[{"p":"On the first cool Saturday of autumn, Dad and I walked to the park with a baseball and two gloves. His glove still had the loose lace. Mine had a patch of worn leather where I’d caught more bad throws than I cared to count."},{"p":"“Ready?” he called."},{"p":"“Depends. Have you inspected your equipment?”"},{"p":"“I’m a librarian, Lincoln. I believe in preventative maintenance through optimism.”"},{"p":"He threw the ball. It sailed a little left. I ran, caught it, and came back grinning."},{"p":"We played until the shadows stretched across third base. Between throws, Dad asked about the advanced lab. I told him I wanted to apply, that the family would need to look at costs and transportation, and that Pike’s reassessment still stood between me and part of the program."},{"p":"“Sounds like several decisions,” he said."},{"p":"“Several forms, too.”"},{"p":"“Now you’re speaking my language.”"},{"p":"I rolled the ball between my palms. “What if I don’t pass?”"},{"p":"Dad didn’t answer right away. A dog barked beyond the fence. Somewhere on the street, somebody was trying to start a lawn mower that clearly had other plans."},{"p":"“Then we’ll know what you need to work on,” he said. “And we’ll decide what comes next with the information we have.”"},{"p":"I groaned. “You’ve all been talking to Mara.”"},{"p":"“Actually, she has been talking to us about your habit of turning every conversation into a flight briefing.”"},{"p":"“That is a serious accusation.”"},{"p":"“It came with supporting documentation.”"},{"p":"My phone buzzed in my pocket. Imani had sent a picture of Nana June’s garden. One tomato was nearly as big as her fist. Behind it, a crooked handwritten sign read *ASK BEFORE YOU PICK*."},{"p":"I laughed and showed Dad."},{"p":"“Wise woman,” he said."},{"p":"I sent back a picture of the baseball field and a message: *I haven’t picked any tomatoes without authorization.*"},{"p":"A moment later Imani replied: *An important improvement in your decision-making.*"},{"p":"I put the phone away and lifted my glove. We still had time for a few more throws."},{"p":"A small airplane crossed above the far trees. I wondered where it was headed. I watched it until it disappeared into the bright space between two clouds."},{"p":"Then Dad called my name, and the baseball was already coming."},{"p":"I turned toward it."}]},{"t":"Ground School Notes — Finding Your Way","blocks":[{"p":"These notes explore real aviation ideas behind Lincoln’s fictional training. They are for curious readers, not a substitute for flight instruction. Flight planning and flying require current official information, appropriate instruction, and compliance with applicable rules."},{"h":"1. A Chart Is a Collection of Clues"},{"p":"A sectional aeronautical chart can show airports, airspace, terrain, obstacles, and many other features. Pilots learn to read the symbols and compare the chart with what they can actually see. Pilots use current, appropriate charts and check for changes affecting the planned flight. Even a familiar landmark can look different from the air or change over time."},{"p":"**Try it:** Draw a map of a room in your home, marking doors, windows, and furniture. Have someone move one safe object. Which parts of your map remain useful? Which part needs checking?"},{"h":"2. Checkpoints and Position"},{"p":"A visual checkpoint is a recognizable feature that can help a pilot compare an actual position with a planned route. One feature alone may be misleading. Pilots can cross-check using several references, navigation instruments, and available services. If unsure of their position, pilots should use appropriate resources and request help rather than guess."},{"p":"**Try it:** On a paper map, choose three features that would be easy to distinguish from one another. Why might a large river bend be more useful than a small parking lot?"},{"h":"3. Time, Distance, and Wind"},{"p":"A basic planning relationship is **time = distance ÷ groundspeed**. Groundspeed is how fast an aircraft moves over the ground, and it can differ from airspeed because of wind. For example, a fictional 60-nautical-mile leg at an estimated groundspeed of 120 knots would take about 30 minutes, assuming the estimate remains valid. Real flight planning also accounts for climbs, descents, changing conditions, and other operational details."},{"p":"**Try it:** A toy train travels 12 meters in 3 minutes at a steady rate. How long would it take to travel 20 meters at that same rate? What would happen to your estimate if it slowed down?"},{"h":"4. Fuel Is More Than Arithmetic"},{"p":"Pilots consider aircraft-specific performance, usable fuel, expected consumption, wind, routing, and required reserves. They must use the applicable aircraft information and regulations. A calculation can be mathematically correct yet unsafe if the input assumptions are wrong. Lincoln’s worksheets teach him to identify missing inputs instead of inventing them."},{"p":"**Try it:** Plan a family car trip on paper. What information would you need before estimating fuel stops? Why wouldn’t the distance alone be enough?"},{"h":"5. A Diversion Is a New Decision"},{"p":"Changing a destination or route may be necessary because of weather, aircraft considerations, airspace, or other conditions. The closest airport isn’t automatically the most suitable. Instructors teach students to consider the situation and available options, and to ask for help when appropriate. Lincoln’s diversions in this book take place under instructor supervision or in classroom scenarios."},{"p":"**Think about it:** If your family found a road closed, would you automatically take the first side street? What would you want to know about it?"},{"h":"6. Notices and Route Verification"},{"p":"A route can be affected by information not obvious on a chart alone. In real aviation, pilots use current official briefings and applicable notices, including Notices to Airmen (NOTAMs), to check for relevant changes and restrictions. A notice may apply only to certain locations, times, or operations. Pilots must check whether it affects the flight they are planning."},{"p":"**Try it:** Imagine a school map showing a path to the gym, plus a notice saying one hallway is closed on Friday afternoon. Draw two routes. Which one works on Friday? What other information might you need?"},{"h":"7. GPS Helps, but Doesn’t Replace Judgment"},{"p":"Satellite navigation can provide valuable position and route information, but pilots must understand the equipment, verify the route and database information as applicable, and maintain awareness of other navigation references and conditions. An electronic display cannot decide whether weather, airspace, or a destination is suitable simply because it draws a line."},{"p":"**Think about it:** When has a phone’s suggested driving route seemed strange? How did you check whether it made sense?"},{"h":"8. Teamwork Means Sharing the Work—and the Doubts"},{"p":"Flight crews, instructors, air traffic controllers, maintenance professionals, and many others contribute to aviation safety. Good communication includes speaking up when something doesn’t look right and listening carefully when someone else notices a problem. Lincoln’s team learns that a disagreement can improve a plan when everyone focuses on the evidence."},{"p":"**Try it:** Build a paper bridge with two friends. Before testing it, ask each person to identify one possible weakness. Can you improve the design without making the discussion about whose idea was best?"},{"h":"9. Training Has Steps"},{"p":"In the United States, a student pilot must generally be at least 16 to solo an airplane and at least 17 to qualify for a private pilot certificate in an airplane, along with meeting the other applicable requirements. Lincoln is still 15 in Book Four. He can learn, plan, and fly with an instructor, but the fellowship’s invitation does not authorize him to fly alone. Readiness evaluations and training opportunities are not pilot certificates."},{"p":"**Lincoln’s Logbook Challenge:** Make a four-column page labeled *What I know*, *What I checked*, *What I still need*, and *Who can help*. Use it to plan a school project or family activity. The most important column might be the one you haven’t filled in yet."},{"h":"AVIATION NOTE"},{"p":"Aviation procedures, regulations, publications, equipment, and airport information can change. This series is fiction and is not a substitute for flight instruction, current official publications, aircraft operating information, or the judgment of a qualified instructor or pilot in command. Readers interested in learning to fly should use current FAA materials and instruction appropriate to their training."},{"h":"HYPE TEEN FLIGHT ACADEMY"},{"p":"Hype Teen Flight Academy follows Lincoln Burgess and his friends as they grow from curious beginners into young aviators who understand that good flying begins long before takeoff. Across seven books, real aviation concepts become part of stories about friendship, judgment, preparation, setbacks, independence, leadership, and service."},{"p":"1. *Balance Before Control*"},{"p":"2. *Crosswind Decisions*"},{"p":"3. *Weather Minimums*"},{"p":"4. *Cross-Country*"},{"p":"5. *Solo*"},{"p":"6. *Pilot in Command*"},{"p":"7. *The Sky We Share*"}],"links":[{"a":"unit:nav","t":"Navigation + Charts"},{"a":"unit:weather","t":"Weather"},{"a":"unit:engine","t":"Engine + Fuel"},{"a":"unit:adm","t":"Decisions + Risk"}]}],"words":30585}); })();
